{"parentUuid":"7b2e09b0-4779-4785-9e5a-6bd05725230a","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app/workspace","sessionId":"05ba8578-c224-4ab5-8187-30ab2bd383b5","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"indexed-skipping-shamir","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_011U74wdqiAV7cbU9n3UsEdB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01Y353Lyt3XVgSka8D7R1Xy3","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"pdftotext -layout /app/workspace/data/format.pdf -","description":"Extract text from format.pdf","timeout":30000}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":253,"cache_read_input_tokens":49920,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":253,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":24}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXt8gGKTGLnWS77pcc51Y","type":"assistant","uuid":"d4803c49-f2df-4ca3-b400-9d029c56c0e3","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:10:44.993Z"}
{"parentUuid":"d4803c49-f2df-4ca3-b400-9d029c56c0e3","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app/workspace","sessionId":"05ba8578-c224-4ab5-8187-30ab2bd383b5","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"indexed-skipping-shamir","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01Y353Lyt3XVgSka8D7R1Xy3","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (37.8KB). Full output saved to: /logs/agent/sessions/projects/-app-workspace/05ba8578-c224-4ab5-8187-30ab2bd383b5/tool-results/toolu_vrtx_01Y353Lyt3XVgSka8D7R1Xy3.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\nGoogle cluster-usage traces: format +\nschema\nCharles Reiss, John Wilkes, Joseph Hellerstein\nVersion of 2013-05-06, for trace version 2. Revised 2014-11-17 for trace version 2.1.\nStatus: exported outside Google.\nCopyright © 2011 Google Inc. All rights reserved.\n\nTable of contents\n   Introduction\n   Common techniques and fields\n       Obfuscation techniques\n       Time and timestamps\n       Unique identifiers\n       User and job names\n       Resource units\n   Data tables\n       Machines\n           Machine events\n           Machine attributes\n       Jobs and tasks\n           Job and task life cycles and event types\n           Missing event records\n           Job events table\n           Task events table\n       Task constraints\n       Resource usage\n   File format\n   Downloading the data\n   Anomalies\n       Data collection\n       Missing information\n   Appendices\n       Document history\n\n\nIntroduction\nThis document describes the semantics, data format, and schema of usage traces of a\nGoogle compute cell. This document describes version 2.1 of the trace format, and should\nbe read in conjunction with the detailed information provided with a trace.\n\nA Google ​cluster​\n                 is a set of machines, packed into racks, and connected by a\nhigh-bandwidth cluster network. A ​  cell​\n                                         is a set of machines, typically all in a single cluster,\nthat share a common cluster-management management system that allocates work to\nmachines. Work arrives at a cell in the form of ​ jobs​\n                                                      . A job is comprised of one or more\ntasks​\n     , each of which is accompanied by a set of resource requirements used for scheduling\n(packing) the tasks onto machines. Each task represents a Linux program, possibly\nconsisting of multiple processes, to be run on a single machine. Tasks and jobs are\nscheduled onto machines according to the lifecycle described below. Resource requirements\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"f021e625-234f-42e4-93da-7177bb0dc021","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:10:45.603Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"Google cluster-usage traces: format +\nschema\nCharles Reiss, John Wilkes, Joseph Hellerstein\nVersion of 2013-05-06, for trace version 2. Revised 2014-11-17 for trace version 2.1.\nStatus: exported outside Google.\nCopyright © 2011 Google Inc. All rights reserved.\n\nTable of contents\n   Introduction\n   Common techniques and fields\n       Obfuscation techniques\n       Time and timestamps\n       Unique identifiers\n       User and job names\n       Resource units\n   Data tables\n       Machines\n           Machine events\n           Machine attributes\n       Jobs and tasks\n           Job and task life cycles and event types\n           Missing event records\n           Job events table\n           Task events table\n       Task constraints\n       Resource usage\n   File format\n   Downloading the data\n   Anomalies\n       Data collection\n       Missing information\n   Appendices\n       Document history\n\n\nIntroduction\nThis document describes the semantics, data format, and schema of usage traces of a\nGoogle compute cell. This document describes version 2.1 of the trace format, and should\nbe read in conjunction with the detailed information provided with a trace.\n\nA Google ​cluster​\n                 is a set of machines, packed into racks, and connected by a\nhigh-bandwidth cluster network. A ​  cell​\n                                         is a set of machines, typically all in a single cluster,\nthat share a common cluster-management management system that allocates work to\nmachines. Work arrives at a cell in the form of ​ jobs​\n                                                      . A job is comprised of one or more\ntasks​\n     , each of which is accompanied by a set of resource requirements used for scheduling\n(packing) the tasks onto machines. Each task represents a Linux program, possibly\nconsisting of multiple processes, to be run on a single machine. Tasks and jobs are\nscheduled onto machines according to the lifecycle described below. Resource requirements\nand usage data for tasks are derived from information provided by the cell's management\nsystem and the individual machines in the cell.\n\nA single usage trace typically describes several days of the workload on one of these\n\n                                                                                                1\n\fcompute cells. A trace is made up of several ​datasets​\n                                                      . A dataset contains a single ​\n                                                                                    table​\n                                                                                         ,\nindexed by a primary key that typically includes a timestamp. Each dataset is packaged as a\nset of one or more files, each provided in a compressed CSV format. (See ​ below​ for the\ndetails.)\n\nOne of our goals in publishing this trace data is to make visible many of the scheduling\ncomplexities that affect Google’s workload, including the variety of job types, complex\nscheduling constraints on some jobs, mixed hardware types, and user mis-estimation of\nresource consumption. We hope that researchers will find it useful.\n\n\nCommon techniques and fields\nThis section describes the representation and semantics of fields that occur in several\ntables. Note that a trace may not include all the types of data described here.\n\n\nObfuscation techniques\nFor confidentiality reasons, we have obfuscated certain information in the trace. In\nparticular, most free-text fields have been randomly hashed, resource sizes have been\nlinearly transformed (scaled), and certain values have been mapped onto a sorted series.\nWe took care to do this in a consistent way so that the data can still be used in research\nstudies.\n\nHere are the obfuscation transformations that are used for most data types:\n   ● unchanged​     : the values are not obfuscated.\n   ● hashed​   : each value is transformed to an opaque value by means of a keyed\n       cryptographic hash.\n   ● ordered​   : the list of observed (or possible) values is generated and sorted. Then, the\n       items in this list are assigned sequential numbers starting with 0 (i.e., gaps are\n       elided), and the observed values are mapped onto these numbers before adding\n       them to the trace.\n   ● rescaled​   : each value is transformed by dividing by the maximum value that appears\n       anywhere in the trace for that measurement. For example, the maximum observed\n       memory capacity across all machines is used to rescale all memory requests and\n       usage values. (The maximum is used to guarantee no dividing by zero and that no\n       extra information leaks into the result.) This value is then rounded to a granularity of\n       max(1.0/210   , max_value/220\n                    ​​              ​​)​- i.e., no more than ten bits of binary precision or ~1\n       millionth of the maximum value, whichever is larger. Almost all floating point values\n       are transformed in this way.\n   ● special​ : a few values are treated in a special way.\n\n\nTime and timestamps\nEach record has a timestamp, which is in microseconds since 600 seconds before the\nbeginning of the trace period, and recorded as a 64 bit integer (i.e., an event 20 seconds\nafter the start of the trace would have a timestamp=620s).\n\nAdditionally, there are two special time values representing events that did not occur within\nthe trace window:\n    ● A time of 0 represents events that occurred before the beginning of the trace\n       window. Examples: machines that existed at the start of the trace; a task or job that\n       was submitted (or has already been scheduled) before start of the the trace.\n\n                                                                                             2\n\f   ●   A time of 2​ -1 (MAXINT) represents events that occur after the end of the trace\n                  63​\n\n       window (e.g., if there is a data collection failure near the end of the trace window).\n\n\n\n\n                      Mapping of original times to times emitted in the trace.\n\nAs an exception, times in usage measurements are treated slightly differently, because the\nthe maximum measurement length is 300 seconds. We apply the same start-time offset to\nthese times as we do for events inside the trace window; this is enough to ensure a clear\nseparation between events before the trace and other events. Note that a measurement\ninterval may overlap the beginning or end of the trace. Additionally, the best available\nprecision for usage measurements is to the nearest second; we still report these times in\nmicroseconds for consistency.\n\nTimestamps are approximate, although reasonably accurate. They are sourced from multiple\nmachines, and so small disagreements in timestamps may reflect clock drift between\nmachines in the cluster instead of any real scheduling or utilization issue.\n\n\nUnique identifiers\nEvery job and every machine is assigned a unique 64-bit identifier. These IDs are never\nreused; however, machine IDs may stay the same when a machine is removed from the\ncluster and returned. Very rarely, job IDs may stay the same when the same job is stopped,\nreconfigured and restarted. (We are aware of only one such example in our first trace.\nUsually, restarting a job will generate a new job ID but keep the same job name and user\nname.)\n\nTasks are identified by the job ID of their job and a 0-based index within the job. Tasks with\nthe same job ID and task index can be (and frequently are) stopped and be restarted\nwithout assigning a new job ID or index.\n\n\nUser and job names\nUser and job names are hashed and provided as opaque base64-encoded strings that can\nbe tested for equality.\n\nThe ​logical job name​\n                     is a heuristically normalized name of the job that combines data from\nseveral internal name fields, and hashes the result to construct an opaque base64-encoded\nstring (e.g., most numbers in the logical name are replaced with a fixed string). In cases\nwhere unique job names are generated to avoid conflicts, job names generated by different\nexecutions of the same program will usually have the same logical name. MapReduce is an\n\n                                                                                                3\n\fexample of a Google system that frequently generates unique job names.\n\nUsernames in this trace represent Google engineers and services. Production jobs run by\nthe same username are likely to be part of the same external or internal service. Whenever\na single program runs multiple jobs (for example, a MapReduce program spawns both a\nmaster job and worker job), those jobs will almost always run as the same user.\n\n\nResource units\nMost resource utilization measurements and requests have been normalized, including:\n   ● CPU (core count or core-seconds/second)\n   ● memory (bytes)\n   ● disk space (bytes)\n   ● disk time fraction (I/O seconds/second)\n\nFor each of the foregoing, we compute separate normalizations. The normalization is a\nscaling relative to the largest capacity of the resource on any machine in the trace (which is\n1.0). This is true even for disk space and disk time fraction for which we do not provide\nindividual machine capacities in this version.\n\n\nData tables\nBelow we describe the tables that will be provided. Table keys are ​\n                                                                   italicized;​note that some\nkeys are constructed from multiple fields. Reminder: not all traces will include all the types\nof data described here. ​\n                        The columns might occur in a different order, or have\ndifferent names than reported here: the definitive specification of such details can\nbe found in the ​schema.csv​file​\n                                .\n\n\nMachines\nMachines are described by two tables: the m\n                                          ​achine events table ​\n                                                               and the ​\n                                                                       machine attributes\ntable​\n     .\n\nMachine events\nEach machine is described by one or more records in the machine event table. The majority\nof records describe machines that existed at the start of the trace.\n\nMachine events table:\n   1. timestamp\n   2. machine ID\n   3. event type\n   4. platform ID\n   5. capacity: CPU\n   6. capacity: memory\n\nThere are three types of machine events:\n   ● ADD (0): a machine became available to the cluster - all machines in the trace will\n       have an ADD event.\n   ● REMOVE (1): a machine was removed from the cluster. Removals can occur due to\n       failures or maintenance.\n   ● UPDATE (2): a machine available to the cluster had its available resources changed.\n\n\n                                                                                             4\n\fMachine capacities reflect the normalized physical capacity of each machine along each\ndimension. Each dimension (CPU cores, RAM size) is normalized independently. Note that\nnot all of this capacity is available to tasks; for example, the cluster scheduler needs to\nreserve some resources for itself and the operating system.\n\nThe platform ID is an opaque string representing the microarchitecture and chipset version\nof the machine. Different combinations of micro-architectures (e.g. vendor chip code-names\nlike Nehalem or Bulldozer) and memory technologies (e.g. DDR2 versus DDR) will result in\ndifferent platform IDs. Two machines with the same platform ID may have substantially\ndifferent clock rates, memory speeds, core counts, etc.\n\nMachine attributes\nMachine attributes table:\n   1. timestamp\n   2. machine ID\n   3. attribute name:​  an opaque string\n   4. attribute value: either an opaque string or an integer\n   5. attribute deleted: a boolean indicating whether the attribute was deleted\n\nMachine attributes are key-value pairs representing machine properties, such as kernel\nversion, clock speed, presence of an external IP address. Tasks can specify constraints on\nmachine attributes.\n\nMachine attribute values are provided as integers if there are a small number of values for\nthat attribute name or as obscured strings otherwise. In the first case, the values of the\nattributes observed across all machines are recorded and sorted (in numerical order, if all\nare numbers). The first value is mapped to 1, the second to 2, and so on. This strategy is\nthe same as used in a ​prior Google publication​\n                                               of some related data.1 Some attributes have\nno associated values because their presence or absence is the signal (e.g., whether a\nmachine runs a GFS chunkserver); these attributes are given the value 1 if present.\n\n\nJobs and tasks\nJobs and tasks are described by these tables:\n   ● Job events table\n   ● Task events table\n   ● Task constraints table\n\nThe two event tables describe jobs/tasks and their lifecycles. The constraints table\ndescribes task placement constraints that restrict the machines onto which tasks can\nschedule.\n\nUsually all tasks within a job execute exactly the same binary with the same options and\nresource request. Programs that need to run different types of tasks with different resource\nrequirements usually execute as multiple jobs. For example, MapReduce runs masters and\nworkers in separate jobs.\n\nJob and task life cycles and event types\n\n\n1\n ​\n “Modeling and Synthesizing Task Placement Constraints in Google Compute Clusters”, Victor\nChudnovsky, Rasekh Rifaat, Joseph Hellerstein, Bikash Sharma, Chita Das, Symposium on Cloud\nComputing (SoCC), Oct. 2011.\n\n                                                                                              5\n\f                             State transitions for jobs and tasks.\n\nThe diagram above shows a simplified model of the states through which a job or task\nprogresses. Job and task events indicate transitions between these states.\n\nThere are basically two types of events: ones that affect the scheduling state (e.g., a job is\nsubmitted, or it gets scheduled and becomes runnable, or its resource requests are\nupdated), and ones that reflect state changes of a task (e.g., the task exits).\n\nEach job and task event has a value representing the type of event. The state of the job or\ntask after the event can always be determined from this event type. For job or task deaths,\nthe event type also contains information about the cause of the death. Here are the event\n(transition) codes:\n\n   ●   SUBMIT (0): A task or job became eligible for scheduling.\n   ●   SCHEDULE (1): A job or task was scheduled on a machine. (It may not start running\n       immediately due to code-shipping time, etc.) For jobs, this occurs the first time ​\n                                                                                         any\n       task of the job is scheduled on a machine.\n   ●   EVICT(2): A task or job was descheduled because of a higher priority task or job,\n       because the scheduler overcommitted and the actual demand exceeded the machine\n       capacity, because the machine on which it was running became unusable (e.g. taken\n       offline for repairs), or because a disk holding the task’s data was lost.\n   ●   FAIL(3): A task or job was descheduled (or, in rare cases, ceased to be eligible for\n       scheduling while it was pending) due to a task failure.\n   ●   FINISH(4): A task or job completed normally.\n   ●   KILL(5): A task or job was cancelled by the user or a driver program or because\n       another job or task on which this job was dependent died.\n   ●   LOST(6): A task or job was presumably terminated, but a record indicating its\n       termination was missing from our source data.\n   ●   UPDATE_PENDING(7): A task or job’s scheduling class, resource requirements, or\n       constraints were updated while it was waiting to be scheduled.\n   ●   UPDATE_RUNNING(8): A task or job’s scheduling class, resource requirements, or\n       constraints were updated while it was scheduled​   .\n\nThe simplest case is shown by the top path in the diagram above: a job is SUBMITted and\ngets put into a pending queue; soon afterwards, it is SCHEDULEd onto a machine and starts\nrunning; some time later it FINISHes successfully.\n\nIf a task or job that has just EVICTed, FAILed, or KILLed remains runnable, a SUBMIT event\nwill appear immediately after the descheduling events. (I.e., the system tries to restart jobs\nand tasks that have failed.) The actual policy about how many times tasks can be\nrescheduled in spite of non-normal termination varies between jobs. For example, though\n\n                                                                                                 6\n\frare, it is possible for some tasks to be indefinitely descheduled after an EVICT event that\nwas caused by the scheduler not giving the task its requested memory.\n\nThe trace may contain both SUBMIT and SCHEDULE events for the exact same microsecond\nfor a job or task. This means that a job or task was submitted and immediately scheduled,\nor, if the timestamp on the events is 0, that the job or task was submitted and scheduled\nbefore the trace began.\n\nUnfortunately, we are not able to precisely determine the cause of all task deaths; where we\nare uncertain, we map this to a KILL event, which also includes cases where an external\nprogram or a developer took explicit action to terminate it. There is no information about\nwhether a job or task was executing normally in either case.\n\nNote that this is a simplified version of the scheduling system we actually employ, and a few\nof the more sophisticated features have been been mapped down into this model. We do not\nbelieve that any significant fidelity has been lost in this process from the point of view of\nresource-scheduling research.\n\nMissing event records\nOur data sources contain both time-series of job and task changes and periodic snapshots of\njob and task state, so we are able to check for consistency between them. When it seems\nwe are missing an event record, we synthesize a replacement. Similarly, we look for a\nrecord of every job or task that is active at the end of the trace time window, and\nsynthesize a missing record if we don't find one.\n\nSynthesized records have a number (called the “missing info” field) to represent why they\nwere added to the trace:\n   ● SNAPSHOT_BUT_NO_TRANSITION (0): we did not find a record representing the\n      given event, but a later snapshot of the job or task state indicated that the transition\n      must have occurred. The timestamp of the synthesized event is the timestamp of the\n      snapshot.\n   ● NO_SNAPSHOT_OR_TRANSITION (1): we did not find a record representing the\n      given termination event, but the job or task disappeared from later snapshots of\n      cluster states, so it must have been terminated. The timestamp of the synthesized\n      event is a pessimistic upper bound on its actual termination time assuming it could\n      have legitimately been missing from one snapshot.\n   ● EXISTS_BUT_NO_CREATION (2): we did not find a record representing the creation\n      of the given task or job. In this case, we may be missing metadata (job name,\n      resource requests, etc.) about the job or task and we may have placed SCHEDULE or\n      SUBMIT events latter than they actually are.\n\nRecords without missing data have no value (empty string) in the “missing info” field.\n\nJob events table\nThe job events table contains the following fields:\n   1. timestamp\n   2. missing info\n   3. job ID\n   4. event type\n   5. user name\n   6. scheduling class\n   7. job name\n   8. logical job name\n                                                                                               7\n\fThe job/task event tables include any jobs that are active (RUNNING) or eligible to run but\nwaiting to be scheduled (PENDING) at any point in the trace. For every job in the trace, we\nwill include at least one record for all its tasks, which will include its scheduling constraints.\n\nAll jobs and tasks have a ​scheduling class​that roughly represents how latency-sensitive it\nis. The scheduling class is represented by a single number, with 3 representing a more\nlatency-sensitive task (e.g., serving revenue-generating user requests) and 0 representing a\nnon-production task (e.g., development, non-business-critical analyses, etc.). Note that\nscheduling class is ​\n                    not​a priority, although more latency-sensitive tasks tend to have higher\ntask priorities. Scheduling class affects machine-local policy for resource access. Priority\ndetermines whether a task is scheduled on a machine.\n\nThe ​job name​ is hashed and provided as opaque base64-encoded strings that can be tested\nfor equality. Unique job names are sometimes generated by automated systems to avoid\nconflicts. (MapReduce is an example of a Google system that does this.)\n\nThe ​logical job name​is an opaque name that combines data from several internal name\nfields (e.g., most numbers in the logical name are replaced with a fixed string). Logical job\nnames partially compensate for the unique job names generated by automatic tools; in this\ncase, different executions of the same program will usually have the same logical name.\n\nTask events table\nThe task events table contains the following fields:\n   1. timestamp\n   2. missing info\n   3. job ID\n   4. task index​ - within the job\n   5. machine ID\n   6. event type\n   7. user name\n   8. scheduling class\n   9. priority\n   10. resource request for CPU cores\n   11. resource request for RAM\n   12. resource request for local disk space\n   13. different-machine constraint\n\nThe machineID, if present, indicates the machine onto which the task was scheduled.\n\nEach task has a ​priority, ​\n                           a small integer that is mapped here into a sorted set of values,\nwith 0 as the lowest priority (least important). Tasks with larger priority numbers generally\nget preference for resources over tasks with smaller priority numbers.\n\nThere are some special priority ranges:\n   ● “free” priorities: these are the lowest priorities. Resources requested at these\n       priorities incur little internal charging.\n   ● “production” priorities: these are the highest priorities. The cluster scheduler\n       attempts to prevent latency-sensitive tasks at these priorities from being evicted due\n       to over-allocation of machine resources.\n   ● “monitoring” priorities: these priorities are intended for jobs which monitor the\n       health of other, lower-priority jobs\nWe may indicate which priority ranges represent which categories in the documentation for\n\n                                                                                                     8\n\feach trace; since we normalize the provided priorities, these ranges may differ between\nreleases.\n\nIf the ​\n       different-machine constraint​ field is present, and true, it indicates that a task must be\nscheduled to execute on a different machine than any other currently running task in the\njob. It is a special type of constraint.\n\nThe resource requests represent the maximum amount of CPU, memory, or disk space a\ntask is permitted to use (we call this its ​\n                                           limit​\n                                                ). Tasks using more than their limit may be\nthrottled (for resources like CPU) or killed (for resources like memory). Note that the\nscheduler may over-commit resources on a machine. As a result, it can happen that there\nare not enough resources to meet all the runtime requests from the tasks, even though\neach of them is using less than its limit. If this happens, one or more low priority task(s)\nmay be killed.\n\nAdditionally, the runtime environment sometimes permits tasks to use more than their\nrequest. For example, tasks are permitted to use free CPU capacity on the machine, so\ntasks with brief latency-insensitive CPU bursts might usefully run with a requested CPU\nallocation of 0.\n\nThis trace includes some jobs that support a Google distributed filesystem, but which do ​  not\nrequest disk space for the file system’s persistent storage via the cluster scheduler - they\nrely on an external allocation mechanism. These resource requests and usage are not\nincluded in the trace data.\n\n\nTask constraints\nA task may have zero or more task placement constraints, which restrict the machines on\nwhich the task can run. Each constraint record corresponds to exactly one task event\nrecord. (If a task is updated only by updating the machine constraints, then there will be a\ntask event record indicating an update.) There may be multiple constraints on a single\nattribute, for example an attribute may have both a less than and a greater than constraint\nfor a single task.\n\nThe task constraints table contains the following fields:\n   1. timestamp\n   2. job ID\n   3. task index\n   4. attribute name ​ -- corresponds to machine attribute table\n   5. attribute value -- either an opaque string or an integer or the empty string\n   6. comparison operator\n\nThe comparison operators are:\n   ● LESS THAN (2), GREATER THAN (3): Represent the machine attribute as an integer\n      (or 0 if the attribute is not present), then compare it to the supplied attribute value.\n      These comparisons are ​    strictly less than​\n                                                   and ​\n                                                       strictly greater than​\n                                                                            ;\n   ● EQUAL (0), NOT EQUAL (1): Represent the machine attribute as a string (or as the\n      empty string if it is not present), then compare it to the supplied attribute value;\n\n\nResource usage\nOur clusters uses Linux containers for resource isolation and usage accounting. Each task\nruns within its own container and may create multiple processes in that container.\n\n                                                                                                 9\n\fWe report usage values from each measurement period, which is typically 5 minutes (300s),\nalthough some measurement records span a shorter time, typically because the task is\nupdated within that time period. When a task is killed, the measurement time period may\nextend for up to tens of seconds beyond when the task was terminated, and a few\nmeasurement records extend a few minutes after the time the task was terminated.\n\nWithin each measurement period, measurements are usually taken at 1 second intervals,\nalthough system load sometimes prevents the resource information from being sampled at\nthe desired rate, so a measurement may span more than 1 second. To assist in identifying\nthese cases, we provide the ​\n                            sample portion​\n                                          (aka ​sampling rate)​which is the ratio between\nthe number of expected samples (e.g., 300 for a 300 s measurement window) to the\nnumber of observed samples.\n\nThe 1 second samples are aggregated over the length of each measurement period to\nprovide a mean value for the measurement period as well as the maximum value seen in\nany 1 second sample in that period.\n\nIn some cases, the aggregate value for the container is constructed from measurements\nobtained from multiple sub-containers: although we know the maximum usage for each\nsubcontainer, our measurement system may not report the true maximum of the outer\ncontainer. In this case, the maximum value we report is the sum of the maximum values\nfrom the subcontainers, and records with maximum usage aggregated in this manner will be\nmarked with an ​  aggregation type​marker of 1; all other records will have an aggregation\ntype of 0. Additionally, we may not have maximum values for all of the sub-containers\n(e.g., if they are small and/or short-lived), even though the average usage measurement\nincludes their usage.\n\nMeasurement records may be missing. Missing records do not necessarily indicate that a\ntask was not running.\n\nThere are some task usage measurements for periods when no process belonging to the\ntask was running in the task’s container. For example, these measurements might occur\nwhile binaries are being copied to the machine. In this case, memory, CPU, and disk usage\nfor a task may legitimately be 0. In some cases, a task may run with no process for an\nextended period of time.\n\nThe task resource usage table contains these fields:\n   1. start time​ of the measurement period\n   2. end time​  of the measurement period\n   3. job ID\n   4. task index\n   5. machine ID\n   6. mean CPU usage rate\n   7. canonical memory usage\n   8. assigned memory usage\n   9. unmapped page cache memory usage\n   10. total page cache memory usage\n   11. maximum memory usage\n   12. mean disk I/O time\n   13. mean local disk space used\n   14. maximum CPU usage\n   15. maximum disk IO time\n   16. cycles per instruction (CPI)\n                                                                                         10\n\f   17. memory accesses per instruction (MAI)\n   18. sample portion\n   19. aggregation type (1 if maximums from subcontainers were summed)\n   20. sampled CPU usage: mean CPU usage during a random 1s sample in the\n       measurement period ​ (only in v2.1 and later)\n\nCPU usage (also known as CPU rate) is measured in units of CPU-core seconds per second:\nif a task is using two cores all the time, it will be reflected as a usage of 2.0 core-s/s.\n     ● The \"CPU usage - sampled\" field is only provided in version 2.1 and later. It is the\n        average CPU usage during a one-second period that is picked uniformly at random\n        from the 5-minute measurement period used for the other data in the row. For\n        long-running tasks, this data can be used to build a reasonably accurate stochastic\n        model of CPU usage.\n\nSince memory isolation is achieved through Linux containers, some kernel memory usage\non behalf of the task is accounted to the task. Tasks must request sufficient memory to\ninclude such allocations. The following memory usage data are included:\n    ● memory usage: canonical memory usage measurement; the number of user\n       accessible pages, including page cache but excluding some pages marked as stale.\n    ● unmapped page cache memory: Linux page cache (file-backed memory) not mapped\n       into any userspace process;\n    ● page cache memory: total Linux page cache (file-backed memory)\n    ● assigned memory: memory usage based on the memory actually assigned to the\n       container (but not necessarily used)\n    ● maximum memory usage: the maximum value of the canonical memory usage\n       measurement observed over the measurement interval. This value is not available\n       for some tasks.\n\nDisk I/O time is measured using the ​\n                                    blkio​\n                                         subsystem for Linux containers. Usage\nmeasurements are the sum across all disks on the machine, in units of disk-time seconds\nper second.\n\nDisk space recorded in this trace represents runtime local disk capacity usage. Disk usage\nrequired for binaries and other read-only, pre-staged runtime files is ​\n                                                                       not​\n                                                                          included.\nAdditionally, most disk space used by distributed, persistent storage (e.g. GFS, Colossus) is\nnot accounted for in this trace.\n\nCPI and MAI statistics are collected from processor performance counters; not all machines\ncollect this data. Memory accesses are based on measurements of last-level cache misses.\n\n\nFile format\nA trace is a set of datasets, one each for the different tables described above. Each dataset\nis made up of one or more files, each of which is compressed using gzip.\n\nEach file is in CSV format, using Unix-style line endings (ASCII LF). The CSV files have no\nheader. Rows are sorted by the event timestamp, which is always stored in the first column.\n\nThe filenames used match the following patterns (​    represents a zero-padded five-digit\n                                                 ?????​\nnumber, e.g., 00123).\n\n       README\n       MD5SUM\n                                                                                           11\n\f       SHA1SUM\n       SHA256SUM\n       schema.csv\n       job_events/part-?????-of-?????.csv.gz\n       task_events/part-?????-of-?????.csv.gz\n       machine_events/part-?????-of-?????.csv.gz\n       machine_attributes/part-?????-of-?????.csv.gz\n       task_constraints/part-?????-of-?????.csv.gz\n       task_usage/part-?????-of-?????.csv.gz\n\nField formats are as follows:\n     1. If there is no value for a field, then the field will be empty (no quotes).\n     2. Numerical fields are represented as decimal integers (for timestamps, IDs, event\n         types, etc.) or floating point values (for resource utilization numbers) as might be\n         printed out using the C printf format \"%g\".\n     3. Boolean fields are represented as 0 (false) or 1 (true).\n     4. String fields are base-64 encoded values of SHA256 hashes and so will have constant\n         length.\nAll fields have been normalized so they do not contain commas, newlines or quotes. This\nmeans that each line of the CSV file should be parsable by splitting on commas.\n\nLarge tables are split into several consecutive files, numbered in order by time. CSV records\nare never split across files, but the records for a particular job, task, or machine may be\nsplit across files. The same timestamp ranges will be used for all files with the same file\nnumber across the different datasets (e.g., ​ task_events-01234-of-05678.csv.gz​   will contain\nthe same range of timestamps as ​    machine_events-01234-of-05678.csv.gz)​   .\n\n\nDownloading the data\nThe data is stored in ​\n                      Google Storage for Developers​. Each trace uses its own bucket. (As\nan example in what follows, we will use the v2.1 trace from 2011, which is called\nclusterdata-2011-2.) You do not need to have a Google account or sign up for Google\nStorage to download the data.\n\nIncluded with each trace is a SHA256SUM file, which can be used to verify the integrity of a\ndownload, using the sha256sum command from GNU coreutils using a command like\n\n       sha256sum --check SHA256SUM\n\nThere are three ways to download the data: with the GSUtil utilities (this is the\nrecommended approach); via the boto library, or simply using HTTP. Note that an entire\ntrace is likely to be moderately large (a few tens of GB); see the individual trace\ndescriptions for more information.\n\nCommand line: GSUtil\nFirst, download the ​\n                    GSUtil​\n                          software. Then you use a command like\n\n       ​\n       gsutil ls gs://clusterdata-2011-2/\n\nto list the contents of the bucket and a command like\n\n       gsutil cp -R gs://clusterdata-2011-2/ destination-directory\n\n                                                                                             12\n\fto copy the contents of the bucket to your local disk. You can also select individual sets of\nfiles within the bucket using directory names or pattern matches like\n“​\n gs://clusterdata-2011-2/task_usage/*​   ”. If you have a fast connection, you may find it\nhelpful to use the ​\n                   -m​option to download multiple files in parallel. If your download is\ninterrupted, gsutil will resume that download if you attempt to download on top of the\npartially-transferred files.\n\nProgrammatically: boto library\nYou can download the data from Google Storage programmatically using the ​\n                                                                         boto library​\n                                                                                     .\n\nWeb: HTTP\nAdditionally, each file in Google Storage is available using HTTP. For example,\nhttps://commondatastorage.googleapis.com/clusterdata-2011-2/SHA256SUM​     is the URL for the\nSHA256SUM file for the first trace. Unfortunately, there are no directory indices.\n\n\nAnomalies\nThis section lists a few oddities you should know about.\n\n\nData collection\nThis data is primarily derived from monitoring data, collected by periodic remote procedure\ncalls (RPCs). When the monitoring system or cluster gets overloaded, data may not be\ncollected. Additionally, we have deliberately removed some usage data which appears to\nhave been mislabeled due to a bug in the monitoring system. For scheduler events, we have\nsynthesized records to make the data consistent as described above, but some data is still\nlikely to be missing from the trace.\n\nSee the detailed description that comes with a trace to see how many event records and\nresource usage measurements it is missing.\n\n\nMissing information\nOur cluster scheduler supports several features that are not represented in the traces.\nThese include:\n\n   ●   Inter-job dependencies: users can submit jobs specifying that they cannot be run\n       until another job finishes or can only run while another job is running. Our traces\n       show jobs as submitted when any such job dependencies are satisfied. Jobs whose\n       dependencies are never satisfied are not present. Jobs or tasks killed because a\n       related job dies are shown as a KILL event.\n\n   ●   Other resources: some additional resource types and their usage.\n\n   ●   Other constraints: some non-resource scheduling constraints (for example, limiting\n       the number of tasks within a job that can be on the same rack); \"preferred\"\n       constraints, where the task will request to run on a machine with a particular\n       attribute but still be willing to schedule elsewhere.\n\n   ●   Workload not managed by the scheduler: the task usage and machine capacity\n       measurements do not account for some of the workload on the machines, because\n       some systems run outside the control of the cluster scheduler.\n\n                                                                                                13\n\fAppendices\n\nDocument history\nDate         Notes\n\n2014-11-17   Added info about cluster-2011-2.\n\n2014-09-02   Version 2.1: added \"CPU usage (aka rate) - random 1s sample\" to\n             task-usage table\n\n2013.05.06   Added page numbers; highlighted text pointing out that schema.csv is the\n             definitive reference for columns; explained that CPU usage and CPU rate\n             are the same thing.\n\n2012.03.20   Added note about column order being defined by the schema.csv file;\n             fixed the task-constraint table to match the data.\n\n2011.11.08   Added note about gsutil “max open files” restriction.\n\n2011.10.27   First externally-published version.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                   14","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d4803c49-f2df-4ca3-b400-9d029c56c0e3"}
{"parentUuid":"f021e625-234f-42e4-93da-7177bb0dc021","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app/workspace","sessionId":"05ba8578-c224-4ab5-8187-30ab2bd383b5","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"indexed-skipping-shamir","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_017MwUadb2HBRZDpoKaMSxAP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01TNZ8vckzX23tQ37uDTZve5","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/logs/agent/sessions/projects/-app-workspace/05ba8578-c224-4ab5-8187-30ab2bd383b5/tool-results/toolu_vrtx_01Y353Lyt3XVgSka8D7R1Xy3.txt"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":662,"cache_read_input_tokens":50173,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":662,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":25}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXt8gVRVZTmXiC17UA4DV","type":"assistant","uuid":"24f60f86-0c04-4327-9f19-3e09e1c224f2","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:10:47.682Z"}
{"parentUuid":"24f60f86-0c04-4327-9f19-3e09e1c224f2","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app/workspace","sessionId":"05ba8578-c224-4ab5-8187-30ab2bd383b5","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"indexed-skipping-shamir","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01TNZ8vckzX23tQ37uDTZve5","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→Google cluster-usage traces: format +\n     2→schema\n     3→Charles Reiss, John Wilkes, Joseph Hellerstein\n     4→Version of 2013-05-06, for trace version 2. Revised 2014-11-17 for trace version 2.1.\n     5→Status: exported outside Google.\n     6→Copyright © 2011 Google Inc. All rights reserved.\n     7→\n     8→Table of contents\n     9→   Introduction\n    10→   Common techniques and fields\n    11→       Obfuscation techniques\n    12→       Time and timestamps\n    13→       Unique identifiers\n    14→       User and job names\n    15→       Resource units\n    16→   Data tables\n    17→       Machines\n    18→           Machine events\n    19→           Machine attributes\n    20→       Jobs and tasks\n    21→           Job and task life cycles and event types\n    22→           Missing event records\n    23→           Job events table\n    24→           Task events table\n    25→       Task constraints\n    26→       Resource usage\n    27→   File format\n    28→   Downloading the data\n    29→   Anomalies\n    30→       Data collection\n    31→       Missing information\n    32→   Appendices\n    33→       Document history\n    34→\n    35→\n    36→Introduction\n    37→This document describes the semantics, data format, and schema of usage traces of a\n    38→Google compute cell. This document describes version 2.1 of the trace format, and should\n    39→be read in conjunction with the detailed information provided with a trace.\n    40→\n    41→A Google ​cluster​\n    42→                 is a set of machines, packed into racks, and connected by a\n    43→high-bandwidth cluster network. A ​  cell​\n    44→                                         is a set of machines, typically all in a single cluster,\n    45→that share a common cluster-management management system that allocates work to\n    46→machines. Work arrives at a cell in the form of ​ jobs​\n    47→                                                      . A job is comprised of one or more\n    48→tasks​\n    49→     , each of which is accompanied by a set of resource requirements used for scheduling\n    50→(packing) the tasks onto machines. Each task represents a Linux program, possibly\n    51→consisting of multiple processes, to be run on a single machine. Tasks and jobs are\n    52→scheduled onto machines according to the lifecycle described below. Resource requirements\n    53→and usage data for tasks are derived from information provided by the cell's management\n    54→system and the individual machines in the cell.\n    55→\n    56→A single usage trace typically describes several days of the workload on one of these\n    57→\n    58→                                                                                                1\n    59→\fcompute cells. A trace is made up of several ​datasets​\n    60→                                                      . A dataset contains a single ​\n    61→                                                                                    table​\n    62→                                                                                         ,\n    63→indexed by a primary key that typically includes a timestamp. Each dataset is packaged as a\n    64→set of one or more files, each provided in a compressed CSV format. (See ​ below​ for the\n    65→details.)\n    66→\n    67→One of our goals in publishing this trace data is to make visible many of the scheduling\n    68→complexities that affect Google’s workload, including the variety of job types, complex\n    69→scheduling constraints on some jobs, mixed hardware types, and user mis-estimation of\n    70→resource consumption. We hope that researchers will find it useful.\n    71→\n    72→\n    73→Common techniques and fields\n    74→This section describes the representation and semantics of fields that occur in several\n    75→tables. Note that a trace may not include all the types of data described here.\n    76→\n    77→\n    78→Obfuscation techniques\n    79→For confidentiality reasons, we have obfuscated certain information in the trace. In\n    80→particular, most free-text fields have been randomly hashed, resource sizes have been\n    81→linearly transformed (scaled), and certain values have been mapped onto a sorted series.\n    82→We took care to do this in a consistent way so that the data can still be used in research\n    83→studies.\n    84→\n    85→Here are the obfuscation transformations that are used for most data types:\n    86→   ● unchanged​     : the values are not obfuscated.\n    87→   ● hashed​   : each value is transformed to an opaque value by means of a keyed\n    88→       cryptographic hash.\n    89→   ● ordered​   : the list of observed (or possible) values is generated and sorted. Then, the\n    90→       items in this list are assigned sequential numbers starting with 0 (i.e., gaps are\n    91→       elided), and the observed values are mapped onto these numbers before adding\n    92→       them to the trace.\n    93→   ● rescaled​   : each value is transformed by dividing by the maximum value that appears\n    94→       anywhere in the trace for that measurement. For example, the maximum observed\n    95→       memory capacity across all machines is used to rescale all memory requests and\n    96→       usage values. (The maximum is used to guarantee no dividing by zero and that no\n    97→       extra information leaks into the result.) This value is then rounded to a granularity of\n    98→       max(1.0/210   , max_value/220\n    99→                    ​​              ​​)​- i.e., no more than ten bits of binary precision or ~1\n   100→       millionth of the maximum value, whichever is larger. Almost all floating point values\n   101→       are transformed in this way.\n   102→   ● special​ : a few values are treated in a special way.\n   103→\n   104→\n   105→Time and timestamps\n   106→Each record has a timestamp, which is in microseconds since 600 seconds before the\n   107→beginning of the trace period, and recorded as a 64 bit integer (i.e., an event 20 seconds\n   108→after the start of the trace would have a timestamp=620s).\n   109→\n   110→Additionally, there are two special time values representing events that did not occur within\n   111→the trace window:\n   112→    ● A time of 0 represents events that occurred before the beginning of the trace\n   113→       window. Examples: machines that existed at the start of the trace; a task or job that\n   114→       was submitted (or has already been scheduled) before start of the the trace.\n   115→\n   116→                                                                                             2\n   117→\f   ●   A time of 2​ -1 (MAXINT) represents events that occur after the end of the trace\n   118→                  63​\n   119→\n   120→       window (e.g., if there is a data collection failure near the end of the trace window).\n   121→\n   122→\n   123→\n   124→\n   125→                      Mapping of original times to times emitted in the trace.\n   126→\n   127→As an exception, times in usage measurements are treated slightly differently, because the\n   128→the maximum measurement length is 300 seconds. We apply the same start-time offset to\n   129→these times as we do for events inside the trace window; this is enough to ensure a clear\n   130→separation between events before the trace and other events. Note that a measurement\n   131→interval may overlap the beginning or end of the trace. Additionally, the best available\n   132→precision for usage measurements is to the nearest second; we still report these times in\n   133→microseconds for consistency.\n   134→\n   135→Timestamps are approximate, although reasonably accurate. They are sourced from multiple\n   136→machines, and so small disagreements in timestamps may reflect clock drift between\n   137→machines in the cluster instead of any real scheduling or utilization issue.\n   138→\n   139→\n   140→Unique identifiers\n   141→Every job and every machine is assigned a unique 64-bit identifier. These IDs are never\n   142→reused; however, machine IDs may stay the same when a machine is removed from the\n   143→cluster and returned. Very rarely, job IDs may stay the same when the same job is stopped,\n   144→reconfigured and restarted. (We are aware of only one such example in our first trace.\n   145→Usually, restarting a job will generate a new job ID but keep the same job name and user\n   146→name.)\n   147→\n   148→Tasks are identified by the job ID of their job and a 0-based index within the job. Tasks with\n   149→the same job ID and task index can be (and frequently are) stopped and be restarted\n   150→without assigning a new job ID or index.\n   151→\n   152→\n   153→User and job names\n   154→User and job names are hashed and provided as opaque base64-encoded strings that can\n   155→be tested for equality.\n   156→\n   157→The ​logical job name​\n   158→                     is a heuristically normalized name of the job that combines data from\n   159→several internal name fields, and hashes the result to construct an opaque base64-encoded\n   160→string (e.g., most numbers in the logical name are replaced with a fixed string). In cases\n   161→where unique job names are generated to avoid conflicts, job names generated by different\n   162→executions of the same program will usually have the same logical name. MapReduce is an\n   163→\n   164→                                                                                                3\n   165→\fexample of a Google system that frequently generates unique job names.\n   166→\n   167→Usernames in this trace represent Google engineers and services. Production jobs run by\n   168→the same username are likely to be part of the same external or internal service. Whenever\n   169→a single program runs multiple jobs (for example, a MapReduce program spawns both a\n   170→master job and worker job), those jobs will almost always run as the same user.\n   171→\n   172→\n   173→Resource units\n   174→Most resource utilization measurements and requests have been normalized, including:\n   175→   ● CPU (core count or core-seconds/second)\n   176→   ● memory (bytes)\n   177→   ● disk space (bytes)\n   178→   ● disk time fraction (I/O seconds/second)\n   179→\n   180→For each of the foregoing, we compute separate normalizations. The normalization is a\n   181→scaling relative to the largest capacity of the resource on any machine in the trace (which is\n   182→1.0). This is true even for disk space and disk time fraction for which we do not provide\n   183→individual machine capacities in this version.\n   184→\n   185→\n   186→Data tables\n   187→Below we describe the tables that will be provided. Table keys are ​\n   188→                                                                   italicized;​note that some\n   189→keys are constructed from multiple fields. Reminder: not all traces will include all the types\n   190→of data described here. ​\n   191→                        The columns might occur in a different order, or have\n   192→different names than reported here: the definitive specification of such details can\n   193→be found in the ​schema.csv​file​\n   194→                                .\n   195→\n   196→\n   197→Machines\n   198→Machines are described by two tables: the m\n   199→                                          ​achine events table ​\n   200→                                                               and the ​\n   201→                                                                       machine attributes\n   202→table​\n   203→     .\n   204→\n   205→Machine events\n   206→Each machine is described by one or more records in the machine event table. The majority\n   207→of records describe machines that existed at the start of the trace.\n   208→\n   209→Machine events table:\n   210→   1. timestamp\n   211→   2. machine ID\n   212→   3. event type\n   213→   4. platform ID\n   214→   5. capacity: CPU\n   215→   6. capacity: memory\n   216→\n   217→There are three types of machine events:\n   218→   ● ADD (0): a machine became available to the cluster - all machines in the trace will\n   219→       have an ADD event.\n   220→   ● REMOVE (1): a machine was removed from the cluster. Removals can occur due to\n   221→       failures or maintenance.\n   222→   ● UPDATE (2): a machine available to the cluster had its available resources changed.\n   223→\n   224→\n   225→                                                                                             4\n   226→\fMachine capacities reflect the normalized physical capacity of each machine along each\n   227→dimension. Each dimension (CPU cores, RAM size) is normalized independently. Note that\n   228→not all of this capacity is available to tasks; for example, the cluster scheduler needs to\n   229→reserve some resources for itself and the operating system.\n   230→\n   231→The platform ID is an opaque string representing the microarchitecture and chipset version\n   232→of the machine. Different combinations of micro-architectures (e.g. vendor chip code-names\n   233→like Nehalem or Bulldozer) and memory technologies (e.g. DDR2 versus DDR) will result in\n   234→different platform IDs. Two machines with the same platform ID may have substantially\n   235→different clock rates, memory speeds, core counts, etc.\n   236→\n   237→Machine attributes\n   238→Machine attributes table:\n   239→   1. timestamp\n   240→   2. machine ID\n   241→   3. attribute name:​  an opaque string\n   242→   4. attribute value: either an opaque string or an integer\n   243→   5. attribute deleted: a boolean indicating whether the attribute was deleted\n   244→\n   245→Machine attributes are key-value pairs representing machine properties, such as kernel\n   246→version, clock speed, presence of an external IP address. Tasks can specify constraints on\n   247→machine attributes.\n   248→\n   249→Machine attribute values are provided as integers if there are a small number of values for\n   250→that attribute name or as obscured strings otherwise. In the first case, the values of the\n   251→attributes observed across all machines are recorded and sorted (in numerical order, if all\n   252→are numbers). The first value is mapped to 1, the second to 2, and so on. This strategy is\n   253→the same as used in a ​prior Google publication​\n   254→                                               of some related data.1 Some attributes have\n   255→no associated values because their presence or absence is the signal (e.g., whether a\n   256→machine runs a GFS chunkserver); these attributes are given the value 1 if present.\n   257→\n   258→\n   259→Jobs and tasks\n   260→Jobs and tasks are described by these tables:\n   261→   ● Job events table\n   262→   ● Task events table\n   263→   ● Task constraints table\n   264→\n   265→The two event tables describe jobs/tasks and their lifecycles. The constraints table\n   266→describes task placement constraints that restrict the machines onto which tasks can\n   267→schedule.\n   268→\n   269→Usually all tasks within a job execute exactly the same binary with the same options and\n   270→resource request. Programs that need to run different types of tasks with different resource\n   271→requirements usually execute as multiple jobs. For example, MapReduce runs masters and\n   272→workers in separate jobs.\n   273→\n   274→Job and task life cycles and event types\n   275→\n   276→\n   277→1\n   278→ ​\n   279→ “Modeling and Synthesizing Task Placement Constraints in Google Compute Clusters”, Victor\n   280→Chudnovsky, Rasekh Rifaat, Joseph Hellerstein, Bikash Sharma, Chita Das, Symposium on Cloud\n   281→Computing (SoCC), Oct. 2011.\n   282→\n   283→                                                                                              5\n   284→\f                             State transitions for jobs and tasks.\n   285→\n   286→The diagram above shows a simplified model of the states through which a job or task\n   287→progresses. Job and task events indicate transitions between these states.\n   288→\n   289→There are basically two types of events: ones that affect the scheduling state (e.g., a job is\n   290→submitted, or it gets scheduled and becomes runnable, or its resource requests are\n   291→updated), and ones that reflect state changes of a task (e.g., the task exits).\n   292→\n   293→Each job and task event has a value representing the type of event. The state of the job or\n   294→task after the event can always be determined from this event type. For job or task deaths,\n   295→the event type also contains information about the cause of the death. Here are the event\n   296→(transition) codes:\n   297→\n   298→   ●   SUBMIT (0): A task or job became eligible for scheduling.\n   299→   ●   SCHEDULE (1): A job or task was scheduled on a machine. (It may not start running\n   300→       immediately due to code-shipping time, etc.) For jobs, this occurs the first time ​\n   301→                                                                                         any\n   302→       task of the job is scheduled on a machine.\n   303→   ●   EVICT(2): A task or job was descheduled because of a higher priority task or job,\n   304→       because the scheduler overcommitted and the actual demand exceeded the machine\n   305→       capacity, because the machine on which it was running became unusable (e.g. taken\n   306→       offline for repairs), or because a disk holding the task’s data was lost.\n   307→   ●   FAIL(3): A task or job was descheduled (or, in rare cases, ceased to be eligible for\n   308→       scheduling while it was pending) due to a task failure.\n   309→   ●   FINISH(4): A task or job completed normally.\n   310→   ●   KILL(5): A task or job was cancelled by the user or a driver program or because\n   311→       another job or task on which this job was dependent died.\n   312→   ●   LOST(6): A task or job was presumably terminated, but a record indicating its\n   313→       termination was missing from our source data.\n   314→   ●   UPDATE_PENDING(7): A task or job’s scheduling class, resource requirements, or\n   315→       constraints were updated while it was waiting to be scheduled.\n   316→   ●   UPDATE_RUNNING(8): A task or job’s scheduling class, resource requirements, or\n   317→       constraints were updated while it was scheduled​   .\n   318→\n   319→The simplest case is shown by the top path in the diagram above: a job is SUBMITted and\n   320→gets put into a pending queue; soon afterwards, it is SCHEDULEd onto a machine and starts\n   321→running; some time later it FINISHes successfully.\n   322→\n   323→If a task or job that has just EVICTed, FAILed, or KILLed remains runnable, a SUBMIT event\n   324→will appear immediately after the descheduling events. (I.e., the system tries to restart jobs\n   325→and tasks that have failed.) The actual policy about how many times tasks can be\n   326→rescheduled in spite of non-normal termination varies between jobs. For example, though\n   327→\n   328→                                                                                                 6\n   329→\frare, it is possible for some tasks to be indefinitely descheduled after an EVICT event that\n   330→was caused by the scheduler not giving the task its requested memory.\n   331→\n   332→The trace may contain both SUBMIT and SCHEDULE events for the exact same microsecond\n   333→for a job or task. This means that a job or task was submitted and immediately scheduled,\n   334→or, if the timestamp on the events is 0, that the job or task was submitted and scheduled\n   335→before the trace began.\n   336→\n   337→Unfortunately, we are not able to precisely determine the cause of all task deaths; where we\n   338→are uncertain, we map this to a KILL event, which also includes cases where an external\n   339→program or a developer took explicit action to terminate it. There is no information about\n   340→whether a job or task was executing normally in either case.\n   341→\n   342→Note that this is a simplified version of the scheduling system we actually employ, and a few\n   343→of the more sophisticated features have been been mapped down into this model. We do not\n   344→believe that any significant fidelity has been lost in this process from the point of view of\n   345→resource-scheduling research.\n   346→\n   347→Missing event records\n   348→Our data sources contain both time-series of job and task changes and periodic snapshots of\n   349→job and task state, so we are able to check for consistency between them. When it seems\n   350→we are missing an event record, we synthesize a replacement. Similarly, we look for a\n   351→record of every job or task that is active at the end of the trace time window, and\n   352→synthesize a missing record if we don't find one.\n   353→\n   354→Synthesized records have a number (called the “missing info” field) to represent why they\n   355→were added to the trace:\n   356→   ● SNAPSHOT_BUT_NO_TRANSITION (0): we did not find a record representing the\n   357→      given event, but a later snapshot of the job or task state indicated that the transition\n   358→      must have occurred. The timestamp of the synthesized event is the timestamp of the\n   359→      snapshot.\n   360→   ● NO_SNAPSHOT_OR_TRANSITION (1): we did not find a record representing the\n   361→      given termination event, but the job or task disappeared from later snapshots of\n   362→      cluster states, so it must have been terminated. The timestamp of the synthesized\n   363→      event is a pessimistic upper bound on its actual termination time assuming it could\n   364→      have legitimately been missing from one snapshot.\n   365→   ● EXISTS_BUT_NO_CREATION (2): we did not find a record representing the creation\n   366→      of the given task or job. In this case, we may be missing metadata (job name,\n   367→      resource requests, etc.) about the job or task and we may have placed SCHEDULE or\n   368→      SUBMIT events latter than they actually are.\n   369→\n   370→Records without missing data have no value (empty string) in the “missing info” field.\n   371→\n   372→Job events table\n   373→The job events table contains the following fields:\n   374→   1. timestamp\n   375→   2. missing info\n   376→   3. job ID\n   377→   4. event type\n   378→   5. user name\n   379→   6. scheduling class\n   380→   7. job name\n   381→   8. logical job name\n   382→                                                                                               7\n   383→\fThe job/task event tables include any jobs that are active (RUNNING) or eligible to run but\n   384→waiting to be scheduled (PENDING) at any point in the trace. For every job in the trace, we\n   385→will include at least one record for all its tasks, which will include its scheduling constraints.\n   386→\n   387→All jobs and tasks have a ​scheduling class​that roughly represents how latency-sensitive it\n   388→is. The scheduling class is represented by a single number, with 3 representing a more\n   389→latency-sensitive task (e.g., serving revenue-generating user requests) and 0 representing a\n   390→non-production task (e.g., development, non-business-critical analyses, etc.). Note that\n   391→scheduling class is ​\n   392→                    not​a priority, although more latency-sensitive tasks tend to have higher\n   393→task priorities. Scheduling class affects machine-local policy for resource access. Priority\n   394→determines whether a task is scheduled on a machine.\n   395→\n   396→The ​job name​ is hashed and provided as opaque base64-encoded strings that can be tested\n   397→for equality. Unique job names are sometimes generated by automated systems to avoid\n   398→conflicts. (MapReduce is an example of a Google system that does this.)\n   399→\n   400→The ​logical job name​is an opaque name that combines data from several internal name\n   401→fields (e.g., most numbers in the logical name are replaced with a fixed string). Logical job\n   402→names partially compensate for the unique job names generated by automatic tools; in this\n   403→case, different executions of the same program will usually have the same logical name.\n   404→\n   405→Task events table\n   406→The task events table contains the following fields:\n   407→   1. timestamp\n   408→   2. missing info\n   409→   3. job ID\n   410→   4. task index​ - within the job\n   411→   5. machine ID\n   412→   6. event type\n   413→   7. user name\n   414→   8. scheduling class\n   415→   9. priority\n   416→   10. resource request for CPU cores\n   417→   11. resource request for RAM\n   418→   12. resource request for local disk space\n   419→   13. different-machine constraint\n   420→\n   421→The machineID, if present, indicates the machine onto which the task was scheduled.\n   422→\n   423→Each task has a ​priority, ​\n   424→                           a small integer that is mapped here into a sorted set of values,\n   425→with 0 as the lowest priority (least important). Tasks with larger priority numbers generally\n   426→get preference for resources over tasks with smaller priority numbers.\n   427→\n   428→There are some special priority ranges:\n   429→   ● “free” priorities: these are the lowest priorities. Resources requested at these\n   430→       priorities incur little internal charging.\n   431→   ● “production” priorities: these are the highest priorities. The cluster scheduler\n   432→       attempts to prevent latency-sensitive tasks at these priorities from being evicted due\n   433→       to over-allocation of machine resources.\n   434→   ● “monitoring” priorities: these priorities are intended for jobs which monitor the\n   435→       health of other, lower-priority jobs\n   436→We may indicate which priority ranges represent which categories in the documentation for\n   437→\n   438→                                                                                                     8\n   439→\feach trace; since we normalize the provided priorities, these ranges may differ between\n   440→releases.\n   441→\n   442→If the ​\n   443→       different-machine constraint​ field is present, and true, it indicates that a task must be\n   444→scheduled to execute on a different machine than any other currently running task in the\n   445→job. It is a special type of constraint.\n   446→\n   447→The resource requests represent the maximum amount of CPU, memory, or disk space a\n   448→task is permitted to use (we call this its ​\n   449→                                           limit​\n   450→                                                ). Tasks using more than their limit may be\n   451→throttled (for resources like CPU) or killed (for resources like memory). Note that the\n   452→scheduler may over-commit resources on a machine. As a result, it can happen that there\n   453→are not enough resources to meet all the runtime requests from the tasks, even though\n   454→each of them is using less than its limit. If this happens, one or more low priority task(s)\n   455→may be killed.\n   456→\n   457→Additionally, the runtime environment sometimes permits tasks to use more than their\n   458→request. For example, tasks are permitted to use free CPU capacity on the machine, so\n   459→tasks with brief latency-insensitive CPU bursts might usefully run with a requested CPU\n   460→allocation of 0.\n   461→\n   462→This trace includes some jobs that support a Google distributed filesystem, but which do ​  not\n   463→request disk space for the file system’s persistent storage via the cluster scheduler - they\n   464→rely on an external allocation mechanism. These resource requests and usage are not\n   465→included in the trace data.\n   466→\n   467→\n   468→Task constraints\n   469→A task may have zero or more task placement constraints, which restrict the machines on\n   470→which the task can run. Each constraint record corresponds to exactly one task event\n   471→record. (If a task is updated only by updating the machine constraints, then there will be a\n   472→task event record indicating an update.) There may be multiple constraints on a single\n   473→attribute, for example an attribute may have both a less than and a greater than constraint\n   474→for a single task.\n   475→\n   476→The task constraints table contains the following fields:\n   477→   1. timestamp\n   478→   2. job ID\n   479→   3. task index\n   480→   4. attribute name ​ -- corresponds to machine attribute table\n   481→   5. attribute value -- either an opaque string or an integer or the empty string\n   482→   6. comparison operator\n   483→\n   484→The comparison operators are:\n   485→   ● LESS THAN (2), GREATER THAN (3): Represent the machine attribute as an integer\n   486→      (or 0 if the attribute is not present), then compare it to the supplied attribute value.\n   487→      These comparisons are ​    strictly less than​\n   488→                                                   and ​\n   489→                                                       strictly greater than​\n   490→                                                                            ;\n   491→   ● EQUAL (0), NOT EQUAL (1): Represent the machine attribute as a string (or as the\n   492→      empty string if it is not present), then compare it to the supplied attribute value;\n   493→\n   494→\n   495→Resource usage\n   496→Our clusters uses Linux containers for resource isolation and usage accounting. Each task\n   497→runs within its own container and may create multiple processes in that container.\n   498→\n   499→                                                                                                 9\n   500→\fWe report usage values from each measurement period, which is typically 5 minutes (300s),\n   501→although some measurement records span a shorter time, typically because the task is\n   502→updated within that time period. When a task is killed, the measurement time period may\n   503→extend for up to tens of seconds beyond when the task was terminated, and a few\n   504→measurement records extend a few minutes after the time the task was terminated.\n   505→\n   506→Within each measurement period, measurements are usually taken at 1 second intervals,\n   507→although system load sometimes prevents the resource information from being sampled at\n   508→the desired rate, so a measurement may span more than 1 second. To assist in identifying\n   509→these cases, we provide the ​\n   510→                            sample portion​\n   511→                                          (aka ​sampling rate)​which is the ratio between\n   512→the number of expected samples (e.g., 300 for a 300 s measurement window) to the\n   513→number of observed samples.\n   514→\n   515→The 1 second samples are aggregated over the length of each measurement period to\n   516→provide a mean value for the measurement period as well as the maximum value seen in\n   517→any 1 second sample in that period.\n   518→\n   519→In some cases, the aggregate value for the container is constructed from measurements\n   520→obtained from multiple sub-containers: although we know the maximum usage for each\n   521→subcontainer, our measurement system may not report the true maximum of the outer\n   522→container. In this case, the maximum value we report is the sum of the maximum values\n   523→from the subcontainers, and records with maximum usage aggregated in this manner will be\n   524→marked with an ​  aggregation type​marker of 1; all other records will have an aggregation\n   525→type of 0. Additionally, we may not have maximum values for all of the sub-containers\n   526→(e.g., if they are small and/or short-lived), even though the average usage measurement\n   527→includes their usage.\n   528→\n   529→Measurement records may be missing. Missing records do not necessarily indicate that a\n   530→task was not running.\n   531→\n   532→There are some task usage measurements for periods when no process belonging to the\n   533→task was running in the task’s container. For example, these measurements might occur\n   534→while binaries are being copied to the machine. In this case, memory, CPU, and disk usage\n   535→for a task may legitimately be 0. In some cases, a task may run with no process for an\n   536→extended period of time.\n   537→\n   538→The task resource usage table contains these fields:\n   539→   1. start time​ of the measurement period\n   540→   2. end time​  of the measurement period\n   541→   3. job ID\n   542→   4. task index\n   543→   5. machine ID\n   544→   6. mean CPU usage rate\n   545→   7. canonical memory usage\n   546→   8. assigned memory usage\n   547→   9. unmapped page cache memory usage\n   548→   10. total page cache memory usage\n   549→   11. maximum memory usage\n   550→   12. mean disk I/O time\n   551→   13. mean local disk space used\n   552→   14. maximum CPU usage\n   553→   15. maximum disk IO time\n   554→   16. cycles per instruction (CPI)\n   555→                                                                                         10\n   556→\f   17. memory accesses per instruction (MAI)\n   557→   18. sample portion\n   558→   19. aggregation type (1 if maximums from subcontainers were summed)\n   559→   20. sampled CPU usage: mean CPU usage during a random 1s sample in the\n   560→       measurement period ​ (only in v2.1 and later)\n   561→\n   562→CPU usage (also known as CPU rate) is measured in units of CPU-core seconds per second:\n   563→if a task is using two cores all the time, it will be reflected as a usage of 2.0 core-s/s.\n   564→     ● The \"CPU usage - sampled\" field is only provided in version 2.1 and later. It is the\n   565→        average CPU usage during a one-second period that is picked uniformly at random\n   566→        from the 5-minute measurement period used for the other data in the row. For\n   567→        long-running tasks, this data can be used to build a reasonably accurate stochastic\n   568→        model of CPU usage.\n   569→\n   570→Since memory isolation is achieved through Linux containers, some kernel memory usage\n   571→on behalf of the task is accounted to the task. Tasks must request sufficient memory to\n   572→include such allocations. The following memory usage data are included:\n   573→    ● memory usage: canonical memory usage measurement; the number of user\n   574→       accessible pages, including page cache but excluding some pages marked as stale.\n   575→    ● unmapped page cache memory: Linux page cache (file-backed memory) not mapped\n   576→       into any userspace process;\n   577→    ● page cache memory: total Linux page cache (file-backed memory)\n   578→    ● assigned memory: memory usage based on the memory actually assigned to the\n   579→       container (but not necessarily used)\n   580→    ● maximum memory usage: the maximum value of the canonical memory usage\n   581→       measurement observed over the measurement interval. This value is not available\n   582→       for some tasks.\n   583→\n   584→Disk I/O time is measured using the ​\n   585→                                    blkio​\n   586→                                         subsystem for Linux containers. Usage\n   587→measurements are the sum across all disks on the machine, in units of disk-time seconds\n   588→per second.\n   589→\n   590→Disk space recorded in this trace represents runtime local disk capacity usage. Disk usage\n   591→required for binaries and other read-only, pre-staged runtime files is ​\n   592→                                                                       not​\n   593→                                                                          included.\n   594→Additionally, most disk space used by distributed, persistent storage (e.g. GFS, Colossus) is\n   595→not accounted for in this trace.\n   596→\n   597→CPI and MAI statistics are collected from processor performance counters; not all machines\n   598→collect this data. Memory accesses are based on measurements of last-level cache misses.\n   599→\n   600→\n   601→File format\n   602→A trace is a set of datasets, one each for the different tables described above. Each dataset\n   603→is made up of one or more files, each of which is compressed using gzip.\n   604→\n   605→Each file is in CSV format, using Unix-style line endings (ASCII LF). The CSV files have no\n   606→header. Rows are sorted by the event timestamp, which is always stored in the first column.\n   607→\n   608→The filenames used match the following patterns (​    represents a zero-padded five-digit\n   609→                                                 ?????​\n   610→number, e.g., 00123).\n   611→\n   612→       README\n   613→       MD5SUM\n   614→                                                                                           11\n   615→\f       SHA1SUM\n   616→       SHA256SUM\n   617→       schema.csv\n   618→       job_events/part-?????-of-?????.csv.gz\n   619→       task_events/part-?????-of-?????.csv.gz\n   620→       machine_events/part-?????-of-?????.csv.gz\n   621→       machine_attributes/part-?????-of-?????.csv.gz\n   622→       task_constraints/part-?????-of-?????.csv.gz\n   623→       task_usage/part-?????-of-?????.csv.gz\n   624→\n   625→Field formats are as follows:\n   626→     1. If there is no value for a field, then the field will be empty (no quotes).\n   627→     2. Numerical fields are represented as decimal integers (for timestamps, IDs, event\n   628→         types, etc.) or floating point values (for resource utilization numbers) as might be\n   629→         printed out using the C printf format \"%g\".\n   630→     3. Boolean fields are represented as 0 (false) or 1 (true).\n   631→     4. String fields are base-64 encoded values of SHA256 hashes and so will have constant\n   632→         length.\n   633→All fields have been normalized so they do not contain commas, newlines or quotes. This\n   634→means that each line of the CSV file should be parsable by splitting on commas.\n   635→\n   636→Large tables are split into several consecutive files, numbered in order by time. CSV records\n   637→are never split across files, but the records for a particular job, task, or machine may be\n   638→split across files. The same timestamp ranges will be used for all files with the same file\n   639→number across the different datasets (e.g., ​ task_events-01234-of-05678.csv.gz​   will contain\n   640→the same range of timestamps as ​    machine_events-01234-of-05678.csv.gz)​   .\n   641→\n   642→\n   643→Downloading the data\n   644→The data is stored in ​\n   645→                      Google Storage for Developers​. Each trace uses its own bucket. (As\n   646→an example in what follows, we will use the v2.1 trace from 2011, which is called\n   647→clusterdata-2011-2.) You do not need to have a Google account or sign up for Google\n   648→Storage to download the data.\n   649→\n   650→Included with each trace is a SHA256SUM file, which can be used to verify the integrity of a\n   651→download, using the sha256sum command from GNU coreutils using a command like\n   652→\n   653→       sha256sum --check SHA256SUM\n   654→\n   655→There are three ways to download the data: with the GSUtil utilities (this is the\n   656→recommended approach); via the boto library, or simply using HTTP. Note that an entire\n   657→trace is likely to be moderately large (a few tens of GB); see the individual trace\n   658→descriptions for more information.\n   659→\n   660→Command line: GSUtil\n   661→First, download the ​\n   662→                    GSUtil​\n   663→                          software. Then you use a command like\n   664→\n   665→       ​\n   666→       gsutil ls gs://clusterdata-2011-2/\n   667→\n   668→to list the contents of the bucket and a command like\n   669→\n   670→       gsutil cp -R gs://clusterdata-2011-2/ destination-directory\n   671→\n   672→                                                                                             12\n   673→\fto copy the contents of the bucket to your local disk. You can also select individual sets of\n   674→files within the bucket using directory names or pattern matches like\n   675→“​\n   676→ gs://clusterdata-2011-2/task_usage/*​   ”. If you have a fast connection, you may find it\n   677→helpful to use the ​\n   678→                   -m​option to download multiple files in parallel. If your download is\n   679→interrupted, gsutil will resume that download if you attempt to download on top of the\n   680→partially-transferred files.\n   681→\n   682→Programmatically: boto library\n   683→You can download the data from Google Storage programmatically using the ​\n   684→                                                                         boto library​\n   685→                                                                                     .\n   686→\n   687→Web: HTTP\n   688→Additionally, each file in Google Storage is available using HTTP. For example,\n   689→https://commondatastorage.googleapis.com/clusterdata-2011-2/SHA256SUM​     is the URL for the\n   690→SHA256SUM file for the first trace. Unfortunately, there are no directory indices.\n   691→\n   692→\n   693→Anomalies\n   694→This section lists a few oddities you should know about.\n   695→\n   696→\n   697→Data collection\n   698→This data is primarily derived from monitoring data, collected by periodic remote procedure\n   699→calls (RPCs). When the monitoring system or cluster gets overloaded, data may not be\n   700→collected. Additionally, we have deliberately removed some usage data which appears to\n   701→have been mislabeled due to a bug in the monitoring system. For scheduler events, we have\n   702→synthesized records to make the data consistent as described above, but some data is still\n   703→likely to be missing from the trace.\n   704→\n   705→See the detailed description that comes with a trace to see how many event records and\n   706→resource usage measurements it is missing.\n   707→\n   708→\n   709→Missing information\n   710→Our cluster scheduler supports several features that are not represented in the traces.\n   711→These include:\n   712→\n   713→   ●   Inter-job dependencies: users can submit jobs specifying that they cannot be run\n   714→       until another job finishes or can only run while another job is running. Our traces\n   715→       show jobs as submitted when any such job dependencies are satisfied. Jobs whose\n   716→       dependencies are never satisfied are not present. Jobs or tasks killed because a\n   717→       related job dies are shown as a KILL event.\n   718→\n   719→   ●   Other resources: some additional resource types and their usage.\n   720→\n   721→   ●   Other constraints: some non-resource scheduling constraints (for example, limiting\n   722→       the number of tasks within a job that can be on the same rack); \"preferred\"\n   723→       constraints, where the task will request to run on a machine with a particular\n   724→       attribute but still be willing to schedule elsewhere.\n   725→\n   726→   ●   Workload not managed by the scheduler: the task usage and machine capacity\n   727→       measurements do not account for some of the workload on the machines, because\n   728→       some systems run outside the control of the cluster scheduler.\n   729→\n   730→                                                                                                13\n   731→\fAppendices\n   732→\n   733→Document history\n   734→Date         Notes\n   735→\n   736→2014-11-17   Added info about cluster-2011-2.\n   737→\n   738→2014-09-02   Version 2.1: added \"CPU usage (aka rate) - random 1s sample\" to\n   739→             task-usage table\n   740→\n   741→2013.05.06   Added page numbers; highlighted text pointing out that schema.csv is the\n   742→             definitive reference for columns; explained that CPU usage and CPU rate\n   743→             are the same thing.\n   744→\n   745→2012.03.20   Added note about column order being defined by the schema.csv file;\n   746→             fixed the task-constraint table to match the data.\n   747→\n   748→2011.11.08   Added note about gsutil “max open files” restriction.\n   749→\n   750→2011.10.27   First externally-published version.\n   751→\n   752→\n   753→\n   754→\n   755→                                                                                   14\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"uuid":"7523a35f-7a51-4ea5-9723-541cf596f0bb","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:10:47.838Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/logs/agent/sessions/projects/-app-workspace/05ba8578-c224-4ab5-8187-30ab2bd383b5/tool-results/toolu_vrtx_01Y353Lyt3XVgSka8D7R1Xy3.txt","content":"Google cluster-usage traces: format +\nschema\nCharles Reiss, John Wilkes, Joseph Hellerstein\nVersion of 2013-05-06, for trace version 2. Revised 2014-11-17 for trace version 2.1.\nStatus: exported outside Google.\nCopyright © 2011 Google Inc. All rights reserved.\n\nTable of contents\n   Introduction\n   Common techniques and fields\n       Obfuscation techniques\n       Time and timestamps\n       Unique identifiers\n       User and job names\n       Resource units\n   Data tables\n       Machines\n           Machine events\n           Machine attributes\n       Jobs and tasks\n           Job and task life cycles and event types\n           Missing event records\n           Job events table\n           Task events table\n       Task constraints\n       Resource usage\n   File format\n   Downloading the data\n   Anomalies\n       Data collection\n       Missing information\n   Appendices\n       Document history\n\n\nIntroduction\nThis document describes the semantics, data format, and schema of usage traces of a\nGoogle compute cell. This document describes version 2.1 of the trace format, and should\nbe read in conjunction with the detailed information provided with a trace.\n\nA Google ​cluster​\n                 is a set of machines, packed into racks, and connected by a\nhigh-bandwidth cluster network. A ​  cell​\n                                         is a set of machines, typically all in a single cluster,\nthat share a common cluster-management management system that allocates work to\nmachines. Work arrives at a cell in the form of ​ jobs​\n                                                      . A job is comprised of one or more\ntasks​\n     , each of which is accompanied by a set of resource requirements used for scheduling\n(packing) the tasks onto machines. Each task represents a Linux program, possibly\nconsisting of multiple processes, to be run on a single machine. Tasks and jobs are\nscheduled onto machines according to the lifecycle described below. Resource requirements\nand usage data for tasks are derived from information provided by the cell's management\nsystem and the individual machines in the cell.\n\nA single usage trace typically describes several days of the workload on one of these\n\n                                                                                                1\n\fcompute cells. A trace is made up of several ​datasets​\n                                                      . A dataset contains a single ​\n                                                                                    table​\n                                                                                         ,\nindexed by a primary key that typically includes a timestamp. Each dataset is packaged as a\nset of one or more files, each provided in a compressed CSV format. (See ​ below​ for the\ndetails.)\n\nOne of our goals in publishing this trace data is to make visible many of the scheduling\ncomplexities that affect Google’s workload, including the variety of job types, complex\nscheduling constraints on some jobs, mixed hardware types, and user mis-estimation of\nresource consumption. We hope that researchers will find it useful.\n\n\nCommon techniques and fields\nThis section describes the representation and semantics of fields that occur in several\ntables. Note that a trace may not include all the types of data described here.\n\n\nObfuscation techniques\nFor confidentiality reasons, we have obfuscated certain information in the trace. In\nparticular, most free-text fields have been randomly hashed, resource sizes have been\nlinearly transformed (scaled), and certain values have been mapped onto a sorted series.\nWe took care to do this in a consistent way so that the data can still be used in research\nstudies.\n\nHere are the obfuscation transformations that are used for most data types:\n   ● unchanged​     : the values are not obfuscated.\n   ● hashed​   : each value is transformed to an opaque value by means of a keyed\n       cryptographic hash.\n   ● ordered​   : the list of observed (or possible) values is generated and sorted. Then, the\n       items in this list are assigned sequential numbers starting with 0 (i.e., gaps are\n       elided), and the observed values are mapped onto these numbers before adding\n       them to the trace.\n   ● rescaled​   : each value is transformed by dividing by the maximum value that appears\n       anywhere in the trace for that measurement. For example, the maximum observed\n       memory capacity across all machines is used to rescale all memory requests and\n       usage values. (The maximum is used to guarantee no dividing by zero and that no\n       extra information leaks into the result.) This value is then rounded to a granularity of\n       max(1.0/210   , max_value/220\n                    ​​              ​​)​- i.e., no more than ten bits of binary precision or ~1\n       millionth of the maximum value, whichever is larger. Almost all floating point values\n       are transformed in this way.\n   ● special​ : a few values are treated in a special way.\n\n\nTime and timestamps\nEach record has a timestamp, which is in microseconds since 600 seconds before the\nbeginning of the trace period, and recorded as a 64 bit integer (i.e., an event 20 seconds\nafter the start of the trace would have a timestamp=620s).\n\nAdditionally, there are two special time values representing events that did not occur within\nthe trace window:\n    ● A time of 0 represents events that occurred before the beginning of the trace\n       window. Examples: machines that existed at the start of the trace; a task or job that\n       was submitted (or has already been scheduled) before start of the the trace.\n\n                                                                                             2\n\f   ●   A time of 2​ -1 (MAXINT) represents events that occur after the end of the trace\n                  63​\n\n       window (e.g., if there is a data collection failure near the end of the trace window).\n\n\n\n\n                      Mapping of original times to times emitted in the trace.\n\nAs an exception, times in usage measurements are treated slightly differently, because the\nthe maximum measurement length is 300 seconds. We apply the same start-time offset to\nthese times as we do for events inside the trace window; this is enough to ensure a clear\nseparation between events before the trace and other events. Note that a measurement\ninterval may overlap the beginning or end of the trace. Additionally, the best available\nprecision for usage measurements is to the nearest second; we still report these times in\nmicroseconds for consistency.\n\nTimestamps are approximate, although reasonably accurate. They are sourced from multiple\nmachines, and so small disagreements in timestamps may reflect clock drift between\nmachines in the cluster instead of any real scheduling or utilization issue.\n\n\nUnique identifiers\nEvery job and every machine is assigned a unique 64-bit identifier. These IDs are never\nreused; however, machine IDs may stay the same when a machine is removed from the\ncluster and returned. Very rarely, job IDs may stay the same when the same job is stopped,\nreconfigured and restarted. (We are aware of only one such example in our first trace.\nUsually, restarting a job will generate a new job ID but keep the same job name and user\nname.)\n\nTasks are identified by the job ID of their job and a 0-based index within the job. Tasks with\nthe same job ID and task index can be (and frequently are) stopped and be restarted\nwithout assigning a new job ID or index.\n\n\nUser and job names\nUser and job names are hashed and provided as opaque base64-encoded strings that can\nbe tested for equality.\n\nThe ​logical job name​\n                     is a heuristically normalized name of the job that combines data from\nseveral internal name fields, and hashes the result to construct an opaque base64-encoded\nstring (e.g., most numbers in the logical name are replaced with a fixed string). In cases\nwhere unique job names are generated to avoid conflicts, job names generated by different\nexecutions of the same program will usually have the same logical name. MapReduce is an\n\n                                                                                                3\n\fexample of a Google system that frequently generates unique job names.\n\nUsernames in this trace represent Google engineers and services. Production jobs run by\nthe same username are likely to be part of the same external or internal service. Whenever\na single program runs multiple jobs (for example, a MapReduce program spawns both a\nmaster job and worker job), those jobs will almost always run as the same user.\n\n\nResource units\nMost resource utilization measurements and requests have been normalized, including:\n   ● CPU (core count or core-seconds/second)\n   ● memory (bytes)\n   ● disk space (bytes)\n   ● disk time fraction (I/O seconds/second)\n\nFor each of the foregoing, we compute separate normalizations. The normalization is a\nscaling relative to the largest capacity of the resource on any machine in the trace (which is\n1.0). This is true even for disk space and disk time fraction for which we do not provide\nindividual machine capacities in this version.\n\n\nData tables\nBelow we describe the tables that will be provided. Table keys are ​\n                                                                   italicized;​note that some\nkeys are constructed from multiple fields. Reminder: not all traces will include all the types\nof data described here. ​\n                        The columns might occur in a different order, or have\ndifferent names than reported here: the definitive specification of such details can\nbe found in the ​schema.csv​file​\n                                .\n\n\nMachines\nMachines are described by two tables: the m\n                                          ​achine events table ​\n                                                               and the ​\n                                                                       machine attributes\ntable​\n     .\n\nMachine events\nEach machine is described by one or more records in the machine event table. The majority\nof records describe machines that existed at the start of the trace.\n\nMachine events table:\n   1. timestamp\n   2. machine ID\n   3. event type\n   4. platform ID\n   5. capacity: CPU\n   6. capacity: memory\n\nThere are three types of machine events:\n   ● ADD (0): a machine became available to the cluster - all machines in the trace will\n       have an ADD event.\n   ● REMOVE (1): a machine was removed from the cluster. Removals can occur due to\n       failures or maintenance.\n   ● UPDATE (2): a machine available to the cluster had its available resources changed.\n\n\n                                                                                             4\n\fMachine capacities reflect the normalized physical capacity of each machine along each\ndimension. Each dimension (CPU cores, RAM size) is normalized independently. Note that\nnot all of this capacity is available to tasks; for example, the cluster scheduler needs to\nreserve some resources for itself and the operating system.\n\nThe platform ID is an opaque string representing the microarchitecture and chipset version\nof the machine. Different combinations of micro-architectures (e.g. vendor chip code-names\nlike Nehalem or Bulldozer) and memory technologies (e.g. DDR2 versus DDR) will result in\ndifferent platform IDs. Two machines with the same platform ID may have substantially\ndifferent clock rates, memory speeds, core counts, etc.\n\nMachine attributes\nMachine attributes table:\n   1. timestamp\n   2. machine ID\n   3. attribute name:​  an opaque string\n   4. attribute value: either an opaque string or an integer\n   5. attribute deleted: a boolean indicating whether the attribute was deleted\n\nMachine attributes are key-value pairs representing machine properties, such as kernel\nversion, clock speed, presence of an external IP address. Tasks can specify constraints on\nmachine attributes.\n\nMachine attribute values are provided as integers if there are a small number of values for\nthat attribute name or as obscured strings otherwise. In the first case, the values of the\nattributes observed across all machines are recorded and sorted (in numerical order, if all\nare numbers). The first value is mapped to 1, the second to 2, and so on. This strategy is\nthe same as used in a ​prior Google publication​\n                                               of some related data.1 Some attributes have\nno associated values because their presence or absence is the signal (e.g., whether a\nmachine runs a GFS chunkserver); these attributes are given the value 1 if present.\n\n\nJobs and tasks\nJobs and tasks are described by these tables:\n   ● Job events table\n   ● Task events table\n   ● Task constraints table\n\nThe two event tables describe jobs/tasks and their lifecycles. The constraints table\ndescribes task placement constraints that restrict the machines onto which tasks can\nschedule.\n\nUsually all tasks within a job execute exactly the same binary with the same options and\nresource request. Programs that need to run different types of tasks with different resource\nrequirements usually execute as multiple jobs. For example, MapReduce runs masters and\nworkers in separate jobs.\n\nJob and task life cycles and event types\n\n\n1\n ​\n “Modeling and Synthesizing Task Placement Constraints in Google Compute Clusters”, Victor\nChudnovsky, Rasekh Rifaat, Joseph Hellerstein, Bikash Sharma, Chita Das, Symposium on Cloud\nComputing (SoCC), Oct. 2011.\n\n                                                                                              5\n\f                             State transitions for jobs and tasks.\n\nThe diagram above shows a simplified model of the states through which a job or task\nprogresses. Job and task events indicate transitions between these states.\n\nThere are basically two types of events: ones that affect the scheduling state (e.g., a job is\nsubmitted, or it gets scheduled and becomes runnable, or its resource requests are\nupdated), and ones that reflect state changes of a task (e.g., the task exits).\n\nEach job and task event has a value representing the type of event. The state of the job or\ntask after the event can always be determined from this event type. For job or task deaths,\nthe event type also contains information about the cause of the death. Here are the event\n(transition) codes:\n\n   ●   SUBMIT (0): A task or job became eligible for scheduling.\n   ●   SCHEDULE (1): A job or task was scheduled on a machine. (It may not start running\n       immediately due to code-shipping time, etc.) For jobs, this occurs the first time ​\n                                                                                         any\n       task of the job is scheduled on a machine.\n   ●   EVICT(2): A task or job was descheduled because of a higher priority task or job,\n       because the scheduler overcommitted and the actual demand exceeded the machine\n       capacity, because the machine on which it was running became unusable (e.g. taken\n       offline for repairs), or because a disk holding the task’s data was lost.\n   ●   FAIL(3): A task or job was descheduled (or, in rare cases, ceased to be eligible for\n       scheduling while it was pending) due to a task failure.\n   ●   FINISH(4): A task or job completed normally.\n   ●   KILL(5): A task or job was cancelled by the user or a driver program or because\n       another job or task on which this job was dependent died.\n   ●   LOST(6): A task or job was presumably terminated, but a record indicating its\n       termination was missing from our source data.\n   ●   UPDATE_PENDING(7): A task or job’s scheduling class, resource requirements, or\n       constraints were updated while it was waiting to be scheduled.\n   ●   UPDATE_RUNNING(8): A task or job’s scheduling class, resource requirements, or\n       constraints were updated while it was scheduled​   .\n\nThe simplest case is shown by the top path in the diagram above: a job is SUBMITted and\ngets put into a pending queue; soon afterwards, it is SCHEDULEd onto a machine and starts\nrunning; some time later it FINISHes successfully.\n\nIf a task or job that has just EVICTed, FAILed, or KILLed remains runnable, a SUBMIT event\nwill appear immediately after the descheduling events. (I.e., the system tries to restart jobs\nand tasks that have failed.) The actual policy about how many times tasks can be\nrescheduled in spite of non-normal termination varies between jobs. For example, though\n\n                                                                                                 6\n\frare, it is possible for some tasks to be indefinitely descheduled after an EVICT event that\nwas caused by the scheduler not giving the task its requested memory.\n\nThe trace may contain both SUBMIT and SCHEDULE events for the exact same microsecond\nfor a job or task. This means that a job or task was submitted and immediately scheduled,\nor, if the timestamp on the events is 0, that the job or task was submitted and scheduled\nbefore the trace began.\n\nUnfortunately, we are not able to precisely determine the cause of all task deaths; where we\nare uncertain, we map this to a KILL event, which also includes cases where an external\nprogram or a developer took explicit action to terminate it. There is no information about\nwhether a job or task was executing normally in either case.\n\nNote that this is a simplified version of the scheduling system we actually employ, and a few\nof the more sophisticated features have been been mapped down into this model. We do not\nbelieve that any significant fidelity has been lost in this process from the point of view of\nresource-scheduling research.\n\nMissing event records\nOur data sources contain both time-series of job and task changes and periodic snapshots of\njob and task state, so we are able to check for consistency between them. When it seems\nwe are missing an event record, we synthesize a replacement. Similarly, we look for a\nrecord of every job or task that is active at the end of the trace time window, and\nsynthesize a missing record if we don't find one.\n\nSynthesized records have a number (called the “missing info” field) to represent why they\nwere added to the trace:\n   ● SNAPSHOT_BUT_NO_TRANSITION (0): we did not find a record representing the\n      given event, but a later snapshot of the job or task state indicated that the transition\n      must have occurred. The timestamp of the synthesized event is the timestamp of the\n      snapshot.\n   ● NO_SNAPSHOT_OR_TRANSITION (1): we did not find a record representing the\n      given termination event, but the job or task disappeared from later snapshots of\n      cluster states, so it must have been terminated. The timestamp of the synthesized\n      event is a pessimistic upper bound on its actual termination time assuming it could\n      have legitimately been missing from one snapshot.\n   ● EXISTS_BUT_NO_CREATION (2): we did not find a record representing the creation\n      of the given task or job. In this case, we may be missing metadata (job name,\n      resource requests, etc.) about the job or task and we may have placed SCHEDULE or\n      SUBMIT events latter than they actually are.\n\nRecords without missing data have no value (empty string) in the “missing info” field.\n\nJob events table\nThe job events table contains the following fields:\n   1. timestamp\n   2. missing info\n   3. job ID\n   4. event type\n   5. user name\n   6. scheduling class\n   7. job name\n   8. logical job name\n                                                                                               7\n\fThe job/task event tables include any jobs that are active (RUNNING) or eligible to run but\nwaiting to be scheduled (PENDING) at any point in the trace. For every job in the trace, we\nwill include at least one record for all its tasks, which will include its scheduling constraints.\n\nAll jobs and tasks have a ​scheduling class​that roughly represents how latency-sensitive it\nis. The scheduling class is represented by a single number, with 3 representing a more\nlatency-sensitive task (e.g., serving revenue-generating user requests) and 0 representing a\nnon-production task (e.g., development, non-business-critical analyses, etc.). Note that\nscheduling class is ​\n                    not​a priority, although more latency-sensitive tasks tend to have higher\ntask priorities. Scheduling class affects machine-local policy for resource access. Priority\ndetermines whether a task is scheduled on a machine.\n\nThe ​job name​ is hashed and provided as opaque base64-encoded strings that can be tested\nfor equality. Unique job names are sometimes generated by automated systems to avoid\nconflicts. (MapReduce is an example of a Google system that does this.)\n\nThe ​logical job name​is an opaque name that combines data from several internal name\nfields (e.g., most numbers in the logical name are replaced with a fixed string). Logical job\nnames partially compensate for the unique job names generated by automatic tools; in this\ncase, different executions of the same program will usually have the same logical name.\n\nTask events table\nThe task events table contains the following fields:\n   1. timestamp\n   2. missing info\n   3. job ID\n   4. task index​ - within the job\n   5. machine ID\n   6. event type\n   7. user name\n   8. scheduling class\n   9. priority\n   10. resource request for CPU cores\n   11. resource request for RAM\n   12. resource request for local disk space\n   13. different-machine constraint\n\nThe machineID, if present, indicates the machine onto which the task was scheduled.\n\nEach task has a ​priority, ​\n                           a small integer that is mapped here into a sorted set of values,\nwith 0 as the lowest priority (least important). Tasks with larger priority numbers generally\nget preference for resources over tasks with smaller priority numbers.\n\nThere are some special priority ranges:\n   ● “free” priorities: these are the lowest priorities. Resources requested at these\n       priorities incur little internal charging.\n   ● “production” priorities: these are the highest priorities. The cluster scheduler\n       attempts to prevent latency-sensitive tasks at these priorities from being evicted due\n       to over-allocation of machine resources.\n   ● “monitoring” priorities: these priorities are intended for jobs which monitor the\n       health of other, lower-priority jobs\nWe may indicate which priority ranges represent which categories in the documentation for\n\n                                                                                                     8\n\feach trace; since we normalize the provided priorities, these ranges may differ between\nreleases.\n\nIf the ​\n       different-machine constraint​ field is present, and true, it indicates that a task must be\nscheduled to execute on a different machine than any other currently running task in the\njob. It is a special type of constraint.\n\nThe resource requests represent the maximum amount of CPU, memory, or disk space a\ntask is permitted to use (we call this its ​\n                                           limit​\n                                                ). Tasks using more than their limit may be\nthrottled (for resources like CPU) or killed (for resources like memory). Note that the\nscheduler may over-commit resources on a machine. As a result, it can happen that there\nare not enough resources to meet all the runtime requests from the tasks, even though\neach of them is using less than its limit. If this happens, one or more low priority task(s)\nmay be killed.\n\nAdditionally, the runtime environment sometimes permits tasks to use more than their\nrequest. For example, tasks are permitted to use free CPU capacity on the machine, so\ntasks with brief latency-insensitive CPU bursts might usefully run with a requested CPU\nallocation of 0.\n\nThis trace includes some jobs that support a Google distributed filesystem, but which do ​  not\nrequest disk space for the file system’s persistent storage via the cluster scheduler - they\nrely on an external allocation mechanism. These resource requests and usage are not\nincluded in the trace data.\n\n\nTask constraints\nA task may have zero or more task placement constraints, which restrict the machines on\nwhich the task can run. Each constraint record corresponds to exactly one task event\nrecord. (If a task is updated only by updating the machine constraints, then there will be a\ntask event record indicating an update.) There may be multiple constraints on a single\nattribute, for example an attribute may have both a less than and a greater than constraint\nfor a single task.\n\nThe task constraints table contains the following fields:\n   1. timestamp\n   2. job ID\n   3. task index\n   4. attribute name ​ -- corresponds to machine attribute table\n   5. attribute value -- either an opaque string or an integer or the empty string\n   6. comparison operator\n\nThe comparison operators are:\n   ● LESS THAN (2), GREATER THAN (3): Represent the machine attribute as an integer\n      (or 0 if the attribute is not present), then compare it to the supplied attribute value.\n      These comparisons are ​    strictly less than​\n                                                   and ​\n                                                       strictly greater than​\n                                                                            ;\n   ● EQUAL (0), NOT EQUAL (1): Represent the machine attribute as a string (or as the\n      empty string if it is not present), then compare it to the supplied attribute value;\n\n\nResource usage\nOur clusters uses Linux containers for resource isolation and usage accounting. Each task\nruns within its own container and may create multiple processes in that container.\n\n                                                                                                 9\n\fWe report usage values from each measurement period, which is typically 5 minutes (300s),\nalthough some measurement records span a shorter time, typically because the task is\nupdated within that time period. When a task is killed, the measurement time period may\nextend for up to tens of seconds beyond when the task was terminated, and a few\nmeasurement records extend a few minutes after the time the task was terminated.\n\nWithin each measurement period, measurements are usually taken at 1 second intervals,\nalthough system load sometimes prevents the resource information from being sampled at\nthe desired rate, so a measurement may span more than 1 second. To assist in identifying\nthese cases, we provide the ​\n                            sample portion​\n                                          (aka ​sampling rate)​which is the ratio between\nthe number of expected samples (e.g., 300 for a 300 s measurement window) to the\nnumber of observed samples.\n\nThe 1 second samples are aggregated over the length of each measurement period to\nprovide a mean value for the measurement period as well as the maximum value seen in\nany 1 second sample in that period.\n\nIn some cases, the aggregate value for the container is constructed from measurements\nobtained from multiple sub-containers: although we know the maximum usage for each\nsubcontainer, our measurement system may not report the true maximum of the outer\ncontainer. In this case, the maximum value we report is the sum of the maximum values\nfrom the subcontainers, and records with maximum usage aggregated in this manner will be\nmarked with an ​  aggregation type​marker of 1; all other records will have an aggregation\ntype of 0. Additionally, we may not have maximum values for all of the sub-containers\n(e.g., if they are small and/or short-lived), even though the average usage measurement\nincludes their usage.\n\nMeasurement records may be missing. Missing records do not necessarily indicate that a\ntask was not running.\n\nThere are some task usage measurements for periods when no process belonging to the\ntask was running in the task’s container. For example, these measurements might occur\nwhile binaries are being copied to the machine. In this case, memory, CPU, and disk usage\nfor a task may legitimately be 0. In some cases, a task may run with no process for an\nextended period of time.\n\nThe task resource usage table contains these fields:\n   1. start time​ of the measurement period\n   2. end time​  of the measurement period\n   3. job ID\n   4. task index\n   5. machine ID\n   6. mean CPU usage rate\n   7. canonical memory usage\n   8. assigned memory usage\n   9. unmapped page cache memory usage\n   10. total page cache memory usage\n   11. maximum memory usage\n   12. mean disk I/O time\n   13. mean local disk space used\n   14. maximum CPU usage\n   15. maximum disk IO time\n   16. cycles per instruction (CPI)\n                                                                                         10\n\f   17. memory accesses per instruction (MAI)\n   18. sample portion\n   19. aggregation type (1 if maximums from subcontainers were summed)\n   20. sampled CPU usage: mean CPU usage during a random 1s sample in the\n       measurement period ​ (only in v2.1 and later)\n\nCPU usage (also known as CPU rate) is measured in units of CPU-core seconds per second:\nif a task is using two cores all the time, it will be reflected as a usage of 2.0 core-s/s.\n     ● The \"CPU usage - sampled\" field is only provided in version 2.1 and later. It is the\n        average CPU usage during a one-second period that is picked uniformly at random\n        from the 5-minute measurement period used for the other data in the row. For\n        long-running tasks, this data can be used to build a reasonably accurate stochastic\n        model of CPU usage.\n\nSince memory isolation is achieved through Linux containers, some kernel memory usage\non behalf of the task is accounted to the task. Tasks must request sufficient memory to\ninclude such allocations. The following memory usage data are included:\n    ● memory usage: canonical memory usage measurement; the number of user\n       accessible pages, including page cache but excluding some pages marked as stale.\n    ● unmapped page cache memory: Linux page cache (file-backed memory) not mapped\n       into any userspace process;\n    ● page cache memory: total Linux page cache (file-backed memory)\n    ● assigned memory: memory usage based on the memory actually assigned to the\n       container (but not necessarily used)\n    ● maximum memory usage: the maximum value of the canonical memory usage\n       measurement observed over the measurement interval. This value is not available\n       for some tasks.\n\nDisk I/O time is measured using the ​\n                                    blkio​\n                                         subsystem for Linux containers. Usage\nmeasurements are the sum across all disks on the machine, in units of disk-time seconds\nper second.\n\nDisk space recorded in this trace represents runtime local disk capacity usage. Disk usage\nrequired for binaries and other read-only, pre-staged runtime files is ​\n                                                                       not​\n                                                                          included.\nAdditionally, most disk space used by distributed, persistent storage (e.g. GFS, Colossus) is\nnot accounted for in this trace.\n\nCPI and MAI statistics are collected from processor performance counters; not all machines\ncollect this data. Memory accesses are based on measurements of last-level cache misses.\n\n\nFile format\nA trace is a set of datasets, one each for the different tables described above. Each dataset\nis made up of one or more files, each of which is compressed using gzip.\n\nEach file is in CSV format, using Unix-style line endings (ASCII LF). The CSV files have no\nheader. Rows are sorted by the event timestamp, which is always stored in the first column.\n\nThe filenames used match the following patterns (​    represents a zero-padded five-digit\n                                                 ?????​\nnumber, e.g., 00123).\n\n       README\n       MD5SUM\n                                                                                           11\n\f       SHA1SUM\n       SHA256SUM\n       schema.csv\n       job_events/part-?????-of-?????.csv.gz\n       task_events/part-?????-of-?????.csv.gz\n       machine_events/part-?????-of-?????.csv.gz\n       machine_attributes/part-?????-of-?????.csv.gz\n       task_constraints/part-?????-of-?????.csv.gz\n       task_usage/part-?????-of-?????.csv.gz\n\nField formats are as follows:\n     1. If there is no value for a field, then the field will be empty (no quotes).\n     2. Numerical fields are represented as decimal integers (for timestamps, IDs, event\n         types, etc.) or floating point values (for resource utilization numbers) as might be\n         printed out using the C printf format \"%g\".\n     3. Boolean fields are represented as 0 (false) or 1 (true).\n     4. String fields are base-64 encoded values of SHA256 hashes and so will have constant\n         length.\nAll fields have been normalized so they do not contain commas, newlines or quotes. This\nmeans that each line of the CSV file should be parsable by splitting on commas.\n\nLarge tables are split into several consecutive files, numbered in order by time. CSV records\nare never split across files, but the records for a particular job, task, or machine may be\nsplit across files. The same timestamp ranges will be used for all files with the same file\nnumber across the different datasets (e.g., ​ task_events-01234-of-05678.csv.gz​   will contain\nthe same range of timestamps as ​    machine_events-01234-of-05678.csv.gz)​   .\n\n\nDownloading the data\nThe data is stored in ​\n                      Google Storage for Developers​. Each trace uses its own bucket. (As\nan example in what follows, we will use the v2.1 trace from 2011, which is called\nclusterdata-2011-2.) You do not need to have a Google account or sign up for Google\nStorage to download the data.\n\nIncluded with each trace is a SHA256SUM file, which can be used to verify the integrity of a\ndownload, using the sha256sum command from GNU coreutils using a command like\n\n       sha256sum --check SHA256SUM\n\nThere are three ways to download the data: with the GSUtil utilities (this is the\nrecommended approach); via the boto library, or simply using HTTP. Note that an entire\ntrace is likely to be moderately large (a few tens of GB); see the individual trace\ndescriptions for more information.\n\nCommand line: GSUtil\nFirst, download the ​\n                    GSUtil​\n                          software. Then you use a command like\n\n       ​\n       gsutil ls gs://clusterdata-2011-2/\n\nto list the contents of the bucket and a command like\n\n       gsutil cp -R gs://clusterdata-2011-2/ destination-directory\n\n                                                                                             12\n\fto copy the contents of the bucket to your local disk. You can also select individual sets of\nfiles within the bucket using directory names or pattern matches like\n“​\n gs://clusterdata-2011-2/task_usage/*​   ”. If you have a fast connection, you may find it\nhelpful to use the ​\n                   -m​option to download multiple files in parallel. If your download is\ninterrupted, gsutil will resume that download if you attempt to download on top of the\npartially-transferred files.\n\nProgrammatically: boto library\nYou can download the data from Google Storage programmatically using the ​\n                                                                         boto library​\n                                                                                     .\n\nWeb: HTTP\nAdditionally, each file in Google Storage is available using HTTP. For example,\nhttps://commondatastorage.googleapis.com/clusterdata-2011-2/SHA256SUM​     is the URL for the\nSHA256SUM file for the first trace. Unfortunately, there are no directory indices.\n\n\nAnomalies\nThis section lists a few oddities you should know about.\n\n\nData collection\nThis data is primarily derived from monitoring data, collected by periodic remote procedure\ncalls (RPCs). When the monitoring system or cluster gets overloaded, data may not be\ncollected. Additionally, we have deliberately removed some usage data which appears to\nhave been mislabeled due to a bug in the monitoring system. For scheduler events, we have\nsynthesized records to make the data consistent as described above, but some data is still\nlikely to be missing from the trace.\n\nSee the detailed description that comes with a trace to see how many event records and\nresource usage measurements it is missing.\n\n\nMissing information\nOur cluster scheduler supports several features that are not represented in the traces.\nThese include:\n\n   ●   Inter-job dependencies: users can submit jobs specifying that they cannot be run\n       until another job finishes or can only run while another job is running. Our traces\n       show jobs as submitted when any such job dependencies are satisfied. Jobs whose\n       dependencies are never satisfied are not present. Jobs or tasks killed because a\n       related job dies are shown as a KILL event.\n\n   ●   Other resources: some additional resource types and their usage.\n\n   ●   Other constraints: some non-resource scheduling constraints (for example, limiting\n       the number of tasks within a job that can be on the same rack); \"preferred\"\n       constraints, where the task will request to run on a machine with a particular\n       attribute but still be willing to schedule elsewhere.\n\n   ●   Workload not managed by the scheduler: the task usage and machine capacity\n       measurements do not account for some of the workload on the machines, because\n       some systems run outside the control of the cluster scheduler.\n\n                                                                                                13\n\fAppendices\n\nDocument history\nDate         Notes\n\n2014-11-17   Added info about cluster-2011-2.\n\n2014-09-02   Version 2.1: added \"CPU usage (aka rate) - random 1s sample\" to\n             task-usage table\n\n2013.05.06   Added page numbers; highlighted text pointing out that schema.csv is the\n             definitive reference for columns; explained that CPU usage and CPU rate\n             are the same thing.\n\n2012.03.20   Added note about column order being defined by the schema.csv file;\n             fixed the task-constraint table to match the data.\n\n2011.11.08   Added note about gsutil “max open files” restriction.\n\n2011.10.27   First externally-published version.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                   14","numLines":755,"startLine":1,"totalLines":755}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"24f60f86-0c04-4327-9f19-3e09e1c224f2"}
